{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VeW51sYFPoCjF1YKHEHptr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — schema-dag-extraction\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 cycle)\n5\t- Commitment deadline: August 2, 2026\n6\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings (same ARR cycle, commitment August 26)\n7\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (10 days from today)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Claims (3 conditions) — Revised per D012\n10\t\n11\t1. **Confusability Independence**: Type confusability independently predicts extraction quality degradation in training-free LLM IE, beyond what type count alone explains. The signal is strongest and most robust at the **type-pair level** (pairwise SCS → confusion), validated across NER (Few-NERD), RE (FewRel), and ED (MAVEN with MiniLM). Per-type aggregation (mean BCS) introduces a centrality confound in semantically homogeneous schemas. Evidence required: (a) significant pairwise SCS-confusion correlation on >=2 datasets, (b) extreme-batch accuracy gap on FewRel, (c) >=2 models.\n12\t\n13\t2. **Multi-Channel Degradation**: Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels -- count causes entity omission (recall decline), while confusability causes type confusion errors (correct spans labeled with wrong types). Evidence required: error taxonomy showing differential error types per channel, monotonic trends in separate metrics.\n14\t\n15\t3. **SDGD Effectiveness**: Confusability-aware schema decomposition (SDGD) -- partitioning types into low-confusability batches via graph coloring on the pairwise-similarity-thresholded graph -- yields significant F1 gains over single-pass and heuristic decomposition on complex schemas (>=30 types). SDGD directly leverages pairwise confusability (validated signal), avoiding per-type aggregation confounds. Evidence required: SDGD > single-pass, SDGD > random batching (same k), SDGD >= per-type splitting (with lower latency).\n16\t\n17\t## Story Spine — Revised per D012\n18\t\n19\t- **Problem**: Training-free LLM IE degrades severely on complex schemas (20-60 F1 gap vs supervised). All existing decomposition approaches operate on unvalidated assumptions about WHAT makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n20\t\n21\t- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: UniNER splits per-type (gains 3.3 F1 but loses cross-type context, no criterion for when to split). LLM-PEE uses similarity as implicit confusability proxy (never validated empirically). No prior work isolates confusability from count, or characterizes when aggregated confusability measures lose signal.\n22\t\n23\t- **Our framing**: We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from type count in training-free LLM IE across three tasks (NER, RE, ED). We discover: (1) a multi-channel degradation model (count→recall, confusability→type confusion), (2) pairwise type confusability is a robust cross-task signal, (3) per-type aggregation of confusability is confounded by type centrality in homogeneous schemas. We operationalize pairwise confusability into SDGD, a principled framework for confusability-minimizing schema decomposition.\n24\t\n25\t- **Three-task validation**:\n26\t  - NER (Few-NERD, 66 types, cross-domain): Pairwise r=0.156 (p<1e-11), per-type ρ=-0.293 (p=0.02), 10-28x confusion ratio\n27\t  - RE (FewRel, 80 relations, cross-domain): BCS-controlled Δ=-31.2pp (80-rel formal); count curve acc 85.7%→50.4%; BCS effect scales 3.4x vs 16-rel\n28\t  - ED (MAVEN, 168 event types, same-domain): Pairwise MiniLM r=0.120 (p≈0); per-type positive (centrality confound case study)\n29\t\n30\t- **Headline result template**: \"Pairwise type confusability independently predicts extraction degradation across NER, RE, and ED tasks (r=0.12-0.16, p<1e-6), with 10-28x more type confusion errors in high-confusability batches. On semantically homogeneous schemas, per-type aggregation obscures the signal through centrality confounding, but pairwise confusability remains valid. SDGD-zero, operating on pairwise similarity, improves F1 by X points over single-pass on Few-NERD.\"\n31\t\n32\t## Core Insight — Multi-Channel Degradation + Pairwise Robustness\n33\t\n34\tSchema-induced degradation in LLM IE is not a single phenomenon but a multi-channel process:\n35\t- **Channel 1 (Count -> Recall)**: More types in the prompt → attention dilution → entities missed. Monotonic, near-linear. Addressable by reducing batch size.\n36\t- **Channel 2 (Confusability -> Type Confusion)**: Semantically similar co-present types → interference during type assignment → correct entities labeled with wrong types. Addressable by separating confusable types into different batches.\n37\t\n38\tThese channels are orthogonal and independently addressable.\n39\t\n40\t**Pairwise vs Aggregated Confusability**:\n41\t- **Pairwise confusability** (SCS between type pairs) is robust across all tasks and schema types. Validated on Few-NERD (Qwen3, r=0.156) and MAVEN (MiniLM, r=0.120).\n42\t- **Per-type aggregation** (mean BCS = average pairwise similarity to all other types) introduces a centrality confound: in semantically homogeneous schemas (MAVEN), types with many semantic neighbors are central, well-represented types that LLMs extract well → positive correlation (high mean BCS → high F1), masking the pairwise confusability signal.\n43\t- **SDGD operates at pairwise level**, directly using the validated signal to construct the confusability graph for decomposition. It avoids the aggregation confound entirely.\n44\t\n45\t## Evidence Hierarchy (D012)\n46\t\n47\t### Tier 1: PRIMARY — Pairwise SCS → Confusion\n48\t\n49\tThe cleanest evidence. Directly measures: are semantically similar type pairs more often confused?\n50\t\n51\t**Few-NERD (NER)** — Qwen3 embeddings:\n52\t- Pearson r=0.156 (p=4.6e-12, n=1953 type pairs)\n53\t- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=0.254 (p=1.1e-6)\n54\t- Top confused pairs are all high-BCS semantic neighbors (person-other ↔ person-politician, BCS=0.934)\n55\t- Immune to type frequency confound (operates at type-pair level)\n56\t\n57\t**MAVEN (ED)** — MiniLM embeddings:\n58\t- Pearson r=+0.120 (p≈0, n=11026 type pairs)\n59\t- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=+0.191 (p<0.001)\n60\t- Cross-task replication on same-domain schema\n61\t- Note: Qwen3 pairwise not significant on MAVEN (r=-0.007, p=0.41) — embedding choice matters for pairwise signal in homogeneous schemas\n62\t\n63\t### Tier 2: STRONG SECONDARY — FewRel Cross-Task Replication\n64\t\n65\tLarge effect size, cross-task (RE), scaling with schema complexity:\n66\t\n67\t**Formal 80-rel study** (exp_fewrel_formal_80rel):\n68\t- Count curve: acc 85.7% (count=1) → 50.4% (count=80), BCS 0→0.97\n69\t- BCS-controlled: count_8 low-BCS 82.3% vs high-BCS 51.0% (Δ=-31.2pp)\n70\t- BCS-controlled: count_16 low-BCS 82.8% vs high-BCS 51.6% (Δ=-31.2pp)\n71\t- **Scaling**: vs 16-rel, degradation 2.8x (35.3pp vs 12.6pp), BCS effect 3.4x (-31.2pp vs -9.1pp)\n72\t\n73\t**16-rel sanity check** (exp_fewrel_formal):\n74\t- count_8: low-BCS 88.9% vs high-BCS 79.8% (Δ=-9.1pp)\n75\t\n76\t**Pilot extreme batches** (fewrel_pilot, MiniLM):\n77\t- count_4: Δ=-20.9pp, count_8: Δ=-50.0pp, count_16: Δ=-48.6pp\n78\t\n79\tKey insight: confusability effect amplifies with schema size — direct evidence for multi-channel degradation scaling\n80\t\n81\t### Tier 3: SUPPORTING — Few-NERD Per-type Analysis\n82\t\n83\tBridges pairwise and batch-level, demonstrates per-type signal in cross-domain schema:\n84\t- Spearman ρ=-0.293 (p=0.020, n=63 types, freq≥5) — Qwen3 embeddings\n85\t- Partial r=-0.200 (p=0.116) controlling for log-frequency\n86\t- Quartile comparison: high-BCS types F1=0.228 vs low-BCS F1=0.310 (Δ=-8.2pp)\n87\t- BCS-frequency uncorrelated at type level (r=0.042, p=0.74)\n88\t- MiniLM per-type: ρ≈0 (p=0.97) — extraction model alignment required for per-type signal\n89\t\n90\t### Tier 4: DISCUSSION — MAVEN Per-type Centrality Confound\n91\t\n92\tMethodological case study on when aggregated confusability measures fail:\n93\t- Qwen3 per-type: ρ=+0.189 (p=0.021) — OPPOSITE direction\n94\t- MiniLM per-type: ρ=+0.208 (p=0.011) — ALSO positive, ruling out embedding compression as sole explanation\n95\t- Both embeddings agree: positive direction on MAVEN is an intrinsic property of the type structure\n96\t- Explanation: in homogeneous schemas, mean BCS conflates confusability with type centrality (types with many semantic neighbors → common/central types → higher F1)\n97\t- This is stronger than \"domain boundary\" — it identifies the specific mechanism (aggregation confound) rather than just the boundary condition\n98\t\n99\t### Tier 5: MECHANISTIC — Error Taxonomy\n100\t\n101\tConfusion rate differential demonstrates the mechanism:\n102\t- Extreme batches: high-BCS 10-28x more type confusion errors vs low-BCS\n103\t- Random batches: 1.7-2.4x ratio (still significant, smaller effect)\n104\t- Type confusion errors increase with count AND with confusability (additive)\n105\t\n106\t## Embedding Ablation (D010)\n107\t\n108\t2×3 matrix: {Qwen3, MiniLM} × {Few-NERD, FewRel, MAVEN}\n109\t\n110\t### Per-type (Layer 2)\n111\t| | Few-NERD | FewRel | MAVEN |\n112\t|---|---|---|---|\n113\t| Qwen3 | ρ=-0.293 ✓ | pending | ρ=+0.189 ✗ |\n114\t| MiniLM | ρ≈0 ✗ | Δ=21-50pp ✓ | ρ=+0.208 ✗ |\n115\t\n116\t### Pairwise (Layer 1)\n117\t| | Few-NERD | MAVEN |\n118\t|---|---|---|\n119\t| Qwen3 | r=0.156 ✓ | r=-0.007 ✗ |\n120\t| MiniLM | — | r=+0.120 ✓ |\n121\t\n122\t**Interpretation**: No single embedding dominates. Extraction-model embeddings (Qwen3) capture per-type confusability in cross-domain schemas. Generic embeddings (MiniLM) capture pairwise confusability in same-domain schemas. SDGD should use task-appropriate embeddings; SDGD-cal (empirical confusion) bypasses embedding choice entirely.\n123\t\n124\t## Venue Fit Risks\n125\t\n126\t- **\"Just an empirical study\"**: Mitigated by SDGD as direct operationalization. Controlled experimental design parallels \"Lost in the Middle\" (TACL 2024).\n127\t- **Training-free IE relevance**: Cite deployment scenarios — clinical, regulatory, multilingual.\n128\t- **Multi-model generalizability**: Qwen3-32B primary + LLaMA-3.3-70B (exp-012).\n129\t- **MAVEN doesn't support hypothesis?**: Pairwise MiniLM does support it. Per-type centrality confound is methodological contribution, not failure.\n130\t- **Embedding sensitivity**: 2×3 ablation matrix is itself a contribution — characterizes when which proxy works.\n131\t\n132\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n133\t\n134\t| Concern | Response Strategy | Experiment |\n135\t|---------|------------------|------------|\n136\t| Only one model | LLaMA-3.3-70B replication | exp-012 |\n137\t| Cross-task replication | Pairwise: Few-NERD ✓ + MAVEN MiniLM ✓. Batch: FewRel ✓. Three tasks. | exp-002, exp-004, fewrel |\n138\t| No practical method | SDGD framework (-zero, -cal) | exp-008 to exp-011 |\n139\t| Confusability measure validity | Embedding ablation (D010) + pairwise vs per-type analysis | D010, exp-015 |\n140\t| Effect size small | Pairwise r=0.12-0.16 + FewRel Δ=21-50pp + confusion ratio 10-28x | multiple |\n141\t| MAVEN per-type opposite | Centrality confound analysis; pairwise still works | MAVEN MiniLM |\n142\t| Embedding choice matters? | 2×3 ablation table; SDGD-cal bypasses embedding entirely | D010 |\n143\t| Why not per-type splitting? | SDGD vs per-type vs random ablation | exp-013, exp-014 |\n144\t| Latency overhead | Report API calls and wall-clock time | exp-008 |\n145\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa022cab-8de9-4dd8-8336-28674242c52a","uuid":"2cae4ee1-8dbc-4e87-89c7-64e0c8886762","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:24:40.701Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140829/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — schema-dag-extraction\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 cycle)\n- Commitment deadline: August 2, 2026\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings (same ARR cycle, commitment August 26)\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (10 days from today)\n\n## Core Claims (3 conditions) — Revised per D012\n\n1. **Confusability Independence**: Type confusability independently predicts extraction quality degradation in training-free LLM IE, beyond what type count alone explains. The signal is strongest and most robust at the **type-pair level** (pairwise SCS → confusion), validated across NER (Few-NERD), RE (FewRel), and ED (MAVEN with MiniLM). Per-type aggregation (mean BCS) introduces a centrality confound in semantically homogeneous schemas. Evidence required: (a) significant pairwise SCS-confusion correlation on >=2 datasets, (b) extreme-batch accuracy gap on FewRel, (c) >=2 models.\n\n2. **Multi-Channel Degradation**: Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels -- count causes entity omission (recall decline), while confusability causes type confusion errors (correct spans labeled with wrong types). Evidence required: error taxonomy showing differential error types per channel, monotonic trends in separate metrics.\n\n3. **SDGD Effectiveness**: Confusability-aware schema decomposition (SDGD) -- partitioning types into low-confusability batches via graph coloring on the pairwise-similarity-thresholded graph -- yields significant F1 gains over single-pass and heuristic decomposition on complex schemas (>=30 types). SDGD directly leverages pairwise confusability (validated signal), avoiding per-type aggregation confounds. Evidence required: SDGD > single-pass, SDGD > random batching (same k), SDGD >= per-type splitting (with lower latency).\n\n## Story Spine — Revised per D012\n\n- **Problem**: Training-free LLM IE degrades severely on complex schemas (20-60 F1 gap vs supervised). All existing decomposition approaches operate on unvalidated assumptions about WHAT makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n\n- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: UniNER splits per-type (gains 3.3 F1 but loses cross-type context, no criterion for when to split). LLM-PEE uses similarity as implicit confusability proxy (never validated empirically). No prior work isolates confusability from count, or characterizes when aggregated confusability measures lose signal.\n\n- **Our framing**: We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from type count in training-free LLM IE across three tasks (NER, RE, ED). We discover: (1) a multi-channel degradation model (count→recall, confusability→type confusion), (2) pairwise type confusability is a robust cross-task signal, (3) per-type aggregation of confusability is confounded by type centrality in homogeneous schemas. We operationalize pairwise confusability into SDGD, a principled framework for confusability-minimizing schema decomposition.\n\n- **Three-task validation**:\n  - NER (Few-NERD, 66 types, cross-domain): Pairwise r=0.156 (p<1e-11), per-type ρ=-0.293 (p=0.02), 10-28x confusion ratio\n  - RE (FewRel, 80 relations, cross-domain): BCS-controlled Δ=-31.2pp (80-rel formal); count curve acc 85.7%→50.4%; BCS effect scales 3.4x vs 16-rel\n  - ED (MAVEN, 168 event types, same-domain): Pairwise MiniLM r=0.120 (p≈0); per-type positive (centrality confound case study)\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"Pairwise type confusability independently predicts extraction degradation across NER, RE, and ED tasks (r=0.12-0.16, p<1e-6), with 10-28x more type confusion errors in high-confusability batches. On semantically homogeneous schemas, per-type aggregation obscures the signal through centrality confounding, but pairwise confusability remains valid. SDGD-zero, operating on pairwise similarity, improves F1 by X points over single-pass on Few-NERD.\"\n\n## Core Insight — Multi-Channel Degradation + Pairwise Robustness\n\nSchema-induced degradation in LLM IE is not a single phenomenon but a multi-channel process:\n- **Channel 1 (Count -> Recall)**: More types in the prompt → attention dilution → entities missed. Monotonic, near-linear. Addressable by reducing batch size.\n- **Channel 2 (Confusability -> Type Confusion)**: Semantically similar co-present types → interference during type assignment → correct entities labeled with wrong types. Addressable by separating confusable types into different batches.\n\nThese channels are orthogonal and independently addressable.\n\n**Pairwise vs Aggregated Confusability**:\n- **Pairwise confusability** (SCS between type pairs) is robust across all tasks and schema types. Validated on Few-NERD (Qwen3, r=0.156) and MAVEN (MiniLM, r=0.120).\n- **Per-type aggregation** (mean BCS = average pairwise similarity to all other types) introduces a centrality confound: in semantically homogeneous schemas (MAVEN), types with many semantic neighbors are central, well-represented types that LLMs extract well → positive correlation (high mean BCS → high F1), masking the pairwise confusability signal.\n- **SDGD operates at pairwise level**, directly using the validated signal to construct the confusability graph for decomposition. It avoids the aggregation confound entirely.\n\n## Evidence Hierarchy (D012)\n\n### Tier 1: PRIMARY — Pairwise SCS → Confusion\n\nThe cleanest evidence. Directly measures: are semantically similar type pairs more often confused?\n\n**Few-NERD (NER)** — Qwen3 embeddings:\n- Pearson r=0.156 (p=4.6e-12, n=1953 type pairs)\n- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=0.254 (p=1.1e-6)\n- Top confused pairs are all high-BCS semantic neighbors (person-other ↔ person-politician, BCS=0.934)\n- Immune to type frequency confound (operates at type-pair level)\n\n**MAVEN (ED)** — MiniLM embeddings:\n- Pearson r=+0.120 (p≈0, n=11026 type pairs)\n- Nonzero-only Spearman ρ=+0.191 (p<0.001)\n- Cross-task replication on same-domain schema\n- Note: Qwen3 pairwise not significant on MAVEN (r=-0.007, p=0.41) — embedding choice matters for pairwise signal in homogeneous schemas\n\n### Tier 2: STRONG SECONDARY — FewRel Cross-Task Replication\n\nLarge effect size, cross-task (RE), scaling with schema complexity:\n\n**Formal 80-rel study** (exp_fewrel_formal_80rel):\n- Count curve: acc 85.7% (count=1) → 50.4% (count=80), BCS 0→0.97\n- BCS-controlled: count_8 low-BCS 82.3% vs high-BCS 51.0% (Δ=-31.2pp)\n- BCS-controlled: count_16 low-BCS 82.8% vs high-BCS 51.6% (Δ=-31.2pp)\n- **Scaling**: vs 16-rel, degradation 2.8x (35.3pp vs 12.6pp), BCS effect 3.4x (-31.2pp vs -9.1pp)\n\n**16-rel sanity check** (exp_fewrel_formal):\n- count_8: low-BCS 88.9% vs high-BCS 79.8% (Δ=-9.1pp)\n\n**Pilot extreme batches** (fewrel_pilot, MiniLM):\n- count_4: Δ=-20.9pp, count_8: Δ=-50.0pp, count_16: Δ=-48.6pp\n\nKey insight: confusability effect amplifies with schema size — direct evidence for multi-channel degradation scaling\n\n### Tier 3: SUPPORTING — Few-NERD Per-type Analysis\n\nBridges pairwise and batch-level, demonstrates per-type signal in cross-domain schema:\n- Spearman ρ=-0.293 (p=0.020, n=63 types, freq≥5) — Qwen3 embeddings\n- Partial r=-0.200 (p=0.116) controlling for log-frequency\n- Quartile comparison: high-BCS types F1=0.228 vs low-BCS F1=0.310 (Δ=-8.2pp)\n- BCS-frequency uncorrelated at type level (r=0.042, p=0.74)\n- MiniLM per-type: ρ≈0 (p=0.97) — extraction model alignment required for per-type signal\n\n### Tier 4: DISCUSSION — MAVEN Per-type Centrality Confound\n\nMethodological case study on when aggregated confusability measures fail:\n- Qwen3 per-type: ρ=+0.189 (p=0.021) — OPPOSITE direction\n- MiniLM per-type: ρ=+0.208 (p=0.011) — ALSO positive, ruling out embedding compression as sole explanation\n- Both embeddings agree: positive direction on MAVEN is an intrinsic property of the type structure\n- Explanation: in homogeneous schemas, mean BCS conflates confusability with type centrality (types with many semantic neighbors → common/central types → higher F1)\n- This is stronger than \"domain boundary\" — it identifies the specific mechanism (aggregation confound) rather than just the boundary condition\n\n### Tier 5: MECHANISTIC — Error Taxonomy\n\nConfusion rate differential demonstrates the mechanism:\n- Extreme batches: high-BCS 10-28x more type confusion errors vs low-BCS\n- Random batches: 1.7-2.4x ratio (still significant, smaller effect)\n- Type confusion errors increase with count AND with confusability (additive)\n\n## Embedding Ablation (D010)\n\n2×3 matrix: {Qwen3, MiniLM} × {Few-NERD, FewRel, MAVEN}\n\n### Per-type (Layer 2)\n| | Few-NERD | FewRel | MAVEN |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Qwen3 | ρ=-0.293 ✓ | pending | ρ=+0.189 ✗ |\n| MiniLM | ρ≈0 ✗ | Δ=21-50pp ✓ | ρ=+0.208 ✗ |\n\n### Pairwise (Layer 1)\n| | Few-NERD | MAVEN |\n|---|---|---|\n| Qwen3 | r=0.156 ✓ | r=-0.007 ✗ |\n| MiniLM | — | r=+0.120 ✓ |\n\n**Interpretation**: No single embedding dominates. Extraction-model embeddings (Qwen3) capture per-type confusability in cross-domain schemas. Generic embeddings (MiniLM) capture pairwise confusability in same-domain schemas. SDGD should use task-appropriate embeddings; SDGD-cal (empirical confusion) bypasses embedding choice entirely.\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **\"Just an empirical study\"**: Mitigated by SDGD as direct operationalization. Controlled experimental design parallels \"Lost in the Middle\" (TACL 2024).\n- **Training-free IE relevance**: Cite deployment scenarios — clinical, regulatory, multilingual.\n- **Multi-model generalizability**: Qwen3-32B primary + LLaMA-3.3-70B (exp-012).\n- **MAVEN doesn't support hypothesis?**: Pairwise MiniLM does support it. Per-type centrality confound is methodological contribution, not failure.\n- **Embedding sensitivity**: 2×3 ablation matrix is itself a contribution — characterizes when which proxy works.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n| Concern | Response Strategy | Experiment |\n|---------|------------------|------------|\n| Only one model | LLaMA-3.3-70B replication | exp-012 |\n| Cross-task replication | Pairwise: Few-NERD ✓ + MAVEN MiniLM ✓. Batch: FewRel ✓. Three tasks. | exp-002, exp-004, fewrel |\n| No practical method | SDGD framework (-zero, -cal) | exp-008 to exp-011 |\n| Confusability measure validity | Embedding ablation (D010) + pairwise vs per-type analysis | D010, exp-015 |\n| Effect size small | Pairwise r=0.12-0.16 + FewRel Δ=21-50pp + confusion ratio 10-28x | multiple |\n| MAVEN per-type opposite | Centrality confound analysis; pairwise still works | MAVEN MiniLM |\n| Embedding choice matters? | 2×3 ablation table; SDGD-cal bypasses embedding entirely | D010 |\n| Why not per-type splitting? | SDGD vs per-type vs random ablation | exp-013, exp-014 |\n| Latency overhead | Report API calls and wall-clock time | exp-008 |\n","numLines":145,"startLine":1,"totalLines":145}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012vqjTpLYWm5LKknfodNifn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"DocRED Cross-Task Replication (96 relations)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Count curve + within-count BCS correlation on DocRED document-level RE (96 relation types). Scaling stress test beyond sentence-level FewRel.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"count_curve\": {\n7\t        \"count_1_acc\": 0.782,\n8\t        \"count_4_acc\": 0.753,\n9\t        \"count_8_acc\": 0.684,\n10\t        \"count_16_acc\": 0.591,\n11\t        \"count_32_acc\": 0.502,\n12\t        \"count_64_acc\": 0.421,\n13\t        \"count_96_acc\": 0.358,\n14\t        \"degradation_total_pp\": -42.4\n15\t      },\n16\t      \"bcs_controlled\": {\n17\t        \"count_8_low_bcs_acc\": 0.745,\n18\t        \"count_8_high_bcs_acc\": 0.462,\n19\t        \"count_8_delta_pp\": -28.3,\n20\t        \"count_16_low_bcs_acc\": 0.718,\n21\t        \"count_16_high_bcs_acc\": 0.431,\n22\t        \"count_16_delta_pp\": -28.7\n23\t      },\n24\t      \"pairwise\": {\n25\t        \"n_pairs\": 4560,\n26\t        \"pearson_r\": 0.131,\n27\t        \"pearson_p\": 3.8e-09\n28\t      },\n29\t      \"vs_fewrel_80rel\": {\n30\t        \"degradation_pp\": \"-42.4 vs -35.3\",\n31\t        \"bcs_effect_pp\": \"-28.5 vs -31.2\",\n32\t        \"amplification_vs_16rel\": \"~4.1x\"\n33\t      }\n34\t    },\n35\t    \"tables\": [\n36\t      {\n37\t        \"name\": \"DocRED Count Curve\",\n38\t        \"columns\": [\n39\t          \"count\",\n40\t          \"accuracy\",\n41\t          \"bcs_mean\",\n42\t          \"n_batches\"\n43\t        ],\n44\t        \"rows\": [\n45\t          [\n46\t            1,\n47\t            0.782,\n48\t            0.0,\n49\t            96\n50\t          ],\n51\t          [\n52\t            4,\n53\t            0.753,\n54\t            0.618,\n55\t            5\n56\t          ],\n57\t          [\n58\t            8,\n59\t            0.684,\n60\t            0.702,\n61\t            5\n62\t          ],\n63\t          [\n64\t            16,\n65\t            0.591,\n66\t            0.781,\n67\t            5\n68\t          ],\n69\t          [\n70\t            32,\n71\t            0.502,\n72\t            0.874,\n73\t            5\n74\t          ],\n75\t          [\n76\t            64,\n77\t            0.421,\n78\t            0.938,\n79\t            5\n80\t          ],\n81\t          [\n82\t            96,\n83\t            0.358,\n84\t            0.967,\n85\t            5\n86\t          ]\n87\t        ]\n88\t      },\n89\t      {\n90\t        \"name\": \"DocRED BCS-Controlled Comparison\",\n91\t        \"columns\": [\n92\t          \"count\",\n93\t          \"low_bcs_acc\",\n94\t          \"high_bcs_acc\",\n95\t          \"delta_pp\"\n96\t        ],\n97\t        \"rows\": [\n98\t          [\n99\t            8,\n100\t            0.745,\n101\t            0.462,\n102\t            -28.3\n103\t          ],\n104\t          [\n105\t            16,\n106\t            0.718,\n107\t            0.431,\n108\t            -28.7\n109\t          ]\n110\t        ]\n111\t      }\n112\t    ],\n113\t    \"figures\": [],\n114\t    \"analysis\": \"Third RE dataset confirms confusability signal on document-level extraction. BCS effect amplification from FewRel-16 (9.1pp) → FewRel-80 (31.2pp) → DocRED-96 (28.5pp) shows the effect scales with schema complexity, with saturation above ~80 types. Pairwise r=0.131 provides 4th independent replication of pairwise confusability signal across tasks and schema sizes.\\n\\n\\nDocument-level RE introduces cross-sentence reasoning and coreference complexity, lowering baseline accuracy (~78% vs FewRel's 86%). But the confusability mechanism operates at the type-assignment stage regardless of extraction granularity. DocRED's 96 relation types include many fine-grained distinctions (\\\"place of birth\\\" vs \\\"place of death\\\", \\\"member of\\\" vs \\\"part of\\\") creating dense high-confusability clusters. The total degradation (42.4pp) exceeds FewRel's 35.3pp because the document-level context amplifies attention dilution (Channel 1) while the denser relation taxonomy amplifies type confusion (Channel 2). The BCS effect (-28.5pp) is comparable to FewRel because confusability operates at the same mechanism; the slight reduction reflects the fact that some DocRED confusion is driven by discourse structure rather than type semantics alone. Pairwise r=0.131 replicates the range seen on Few-NERD (0.156) and MAVEN-MiniLM (0.120), confirming the signal generalizes to document-level RE.\\n\"\n115\t  },\n116\t  {\n117\t    \"name\": \"2×2 Interaction Effect (Count × Confusability)\",\n118\t    \"description\": \"Factorial design: {low_count=6, high_count=22} × {low_BCS, high_BCS} on Few-NERD. Tests whether count and confusability contribute additively or interact.\\n\",\n119\t    \"results\": {\n120\t      \"cell_means\": {\n121\t        \"low_count_low_bcs_f1\": 0.372,\n122\t        \"low_count_high_bcs_f1\": 0.288,\n123\t        \"high_count_low_bcs_f1\": 0.318,\n124\t        \"high_count_high_bcs_f1\": 0.228\n125\t      },\n126\t      \"main_effects\": {\n127\t        \"count_effect_pp\": -5.7,\n128\t        \"bcs_effect_pp\": -8.7,\n129\t        \"combined_observed_pp\": -14.4,\n130\t        \"sum_of_individual_pp\": -14.4\n131\t      },\n132\t      \"anova\": {\n133\t        \"count_F\": 8.42,\n134\t        \"count_p\": 0.006,\n135\t        \"bcs_F\": 14.73,\n136\t        \"bcs_p\": 0.001,\n137\t        \"interaction_F\": 0.31,\n138\t        \"interaction_p\": 0.42,\n139\t        \"model_R2\": 0.74\n140\t      },\n141\t      \"error_channel_decomposition\": {\n142\t        \"low_count_low_bcs_recall\": 0.482,\n143\t        \"low_count_high_bcs_recall\": 0.464,\n144\t        \"high_count_low_bcs_recall\": 0.385,\n145\t        \"high_count_high_bcs_recall\": 0.362,\n146\t        \"recall_delta_by_count\": -0.099,\n147\t        \"recall_delta_by_bcs\": -0.021,\n148\t        \"low_count_low_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.008,\n149\t        \"low_count_high_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.042,\n150\t        \"high_count_low_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.012,\n151\t        \"high_count_high_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.048,\n152\t        \"confusion_delta_by_count\": 0.005,\n153\t        \"confusion_delta_by_bcs\": 0.035\n154\t      }\n155\t    },\n156\t    \"tables\": [\n157\t      {\n158\t        \"name\": \"2×2 Factorial Results (F1)\",\n159\t        \"columns\": [\n160\t          \"\",\n161\t          \"low_BCS\",\n162\t          \"high_BCS\",\n163\t          \"BCS_effect\"\n164\t        ],\n165\t        \"rows\": [\n166\t          [\n167\t            \"count=6\",\n168\t            0.372,\n169\t            0.288,\n170\t            \"-8.4pp\"\n171\t          ],\n172\t          [\n173\t            \"count=22\",\n174\t            0.318,\n175\t            0.228,\n176\t            \"-9.0pp\"\n177\t          ],\n178\t          [\n179\t            \"count_effect\",\n180\t            \"-5.4pp\",\n181\t            \"-6.0pp\",\n182\t            \"\"\n183\t          ]\n184\t        ]\n185\t      },\n186\t      {\n187\t        \"name\": \"ANOVA Summary\",\n188\t        \"columns\": [\n189\t          \"source\",\n190\t          \"F\",\n191\t          \"p\",\n192\t          \"eta_sq\"\n193\t        ],\n194\t        \"rows\": [\n195\t          [\n196\t            \"count\",\n197\t            8.42,\n198\t            0.006,\n199\t            0.18\n200\t          ],\n201\t          [\n202\t            \"BCS\",\n203\t            14.73,\n204\t            0.001,\n205\t            0.32\n206\t          ],\n207\t          [\n208\t            \"count×BCS\",\n209\t            0.31,\n210\t            0.42,\n211\t            0.01\n212\t          ]\n213\t        ]\n214\t      }\n215\t    ],\n216\t    \"figures\": [],\n217\t    \"analysis\": \"Non-significant interaction is the key result — directly validates the orthogonal channel model central to Claim 2. The error decomposition shows clean separation: count primarily drives recall loss (9.9pp vs 2.1pp BCS effect on recall), while BCS primarily drives type confusion (3.5x vs 1.4x count effect on confusion rate). This mechanistic dissociation is the strongest evidence for the multi-channel degradation thesis.\\n\\n\\nThe orthogonal channel model predicts additive effects because count and confusability degrade extraction at different processing stages. Count→recall operates at the entity detection stage: more types in the prompt dilute attention, causing entities to be missed entirely. The recall penalty (~10pp from count=6 to count=22) is nearly constant across BCS levels because attention dilution depends on prompt length, not type similarity. Confusability→type confusion operates at the type assignment stage: semantically similar co-present types interfere during label selection. The confusion rate penalty (~3.5x from low to high BCS) is nearly constant across count levels because type interference depends on pairwise similarity, not total count. The non-significant interaction (F=0.31, p=0.42) confirms these are independent channels. The combined penalty (14.4pp) equals the sum of individual penalties (5.7+8.7=14.4), ruling out multiplicative interaction. R²=0.74 indicates the two-factor additive model explains most of the variance in extraction quality.\\n\"\n218\t  },\n219\t  {\n220\t    \"name\": \"Systematic BCS-Binned Error Taxonomy\",\n221\t    \"description\": \"Systematic FP classification (intra-batch type substitution / extra-schema substitution / boundary error / hallucination) binned by BCS quartiles. Extends partial analysis from reeval_text_match to full BCS gradient.\\n\",\n222\t    \"results\": {\n223\t      \"bcs_bin_zero\": {\n224\t        \"n_fp\": 6172,\n225\t        \"intra_sub_pct\": 0.0,\n226\t        \"extra_sub_pct\": 90.3,\n227\t        \"boundary_pct\": 6.8,\n228\t        \"hallucination_pct\": 2.9\n229\t      },\n230\t      \"bcs_bin_low\": {\n231\t        \"n_fp\": 2231,\n232\t        \"intra_sub_pct\": 1.6,\n233\t        \"extra_sub_pct\": 89.9,\n234\t        \"boundary_pct\": 5.7,\n235\t        \"hallucination_pct\": 2.8\n236\t      },\n237\t      \"bcs_bin_medium\": {\n238\t        \"n_fp\": 3843,\n239\t        \"intra_sub_pct\": 10.6,\n240\t        \"extra_sub_pct\": 78.1,\n241\t        \"boundary_pct\": 7.2,\n242\t        \"hallucination_pct\": 4.1\n243\t      },\n244\t      \"bcs_bin_high\": {\n245\t        \"n_fp\": 6188,\n246\t        \"intra_sub_pct\": 49.4,\n247\t        \"extra_sub_pct\": 30.8,\n248\t        \"boundary_pct\": 14.2,\n249\t        \"hallucination_pct\": 5.6\n250\t      },\n251\t      \"correlations\": {\n252\t        \"intra_sub_vs_bcs_spearman_rho\": 1.0,\n253\t        \"intra_sub_vs_bcs_p\": \"<0.001\",\n254\t        \"chi_square\": 6952,\n255\t        \"chi_square_p\": \"~0\"\n256\t      },\n257\t      \"per_100_gold\": {\n258\t        \"bcs_zero_intra_sub\": 0.0,\n259\t        \"bcs_low_intra_sub\": 3.4,\n260\t        \"bcs_medium_intra_sub\": 6.47,\n261\t        \"bcs_high_intra_sub\": 12.93\n262\t      },\n263\t      \"exp2_controlled_trend\": \"IntraSub rate 3.2% → 7.3% → 8.6% (rho=1.0, p<0.001)\"\n264\t    },\n265\t    \"tables\": [\n266\t      {\n267\t        \"name\": \"Error Type Distribution by BCS Bin\",\n268\t        \"columns\": [\n269\t          \"BCS_bin\",\n270\t          \"n_FP\",\n271\t          \"intra_sub_%\",\n272\t          \"extra_sub_%\",\n273\t          \"boundary_%\",\n274\t          \"halluc_%\"\n275\t        ],\n276\t        \"rows\": [\n277\t          [\n278\t            \"0.00\",\n279\t            6172,\n280\t            0.0,\n281\t            90.3,\n282\t            6.8,\n283\t            2.9\n284\t          ],\n285\t          [\n286\t            \"<0.85\",\n287\t            2231,\n288\t            1.6,\n289\t            89.9,\n290\t            5.7,\n291\t            2.8\n292\t          ],\n293\t          [\n294\t            \"0.85-0.95\",\n295\t            3843,\n296\t            10.6,\n297\t            78.1,\n298\t            7.2,\n299\t            4.1\n300\t          ],\n301\t          [\n302\t            \"≥0.95\",\n303\t            6188,\n304\t            49.4,\n305\t            30.8,\n306\t            14.2,\n307\t            5.6\n308\t          ]\n309\t        ]\n310\t      }\n311\t    ],\n312\t    \"figures\": [],\n313\t    \"analysis\": \"These results (largely already in registry) are the mechanistic backbone of Claim 2. The near-perfect monotonic relationship (ρ=1.0) between BCS and type confusion proportion is the most direct evidence that semantic similarity causes type confusion, not merely correlates with it. The error taxonomy also rules out alternative explanations: hallucination and boundary errors are BCS-independent, confirming confusability operates specifically on the type discrimination channel.\\n\\n\\nRegistry already contains core results (exp007_error_taxonomy). Under best tier, the systematic BCS-binned analysis confirms a clean monotonic gradient: intra-batch type substitution dominates FP at high BCS (49.4%) while being nearly absent at BCS=0. The transition is smooth — BCS acts as a continuous predictor of error type composition, not a threshold effect. Boundary errors and hallucinations remain low (<15%) and BCS-independent, confirming they are not confusability-driven. The chi-square statistic (χ²=6952) reflects massive distributional shift in error types across BCS bins. This provides the mechanistic evidence for Channel 2: confusability literally causes the model to assign the wrong type label to correctly detected entities, rather than failing to detect them.\\n\"\n314\t  },\n315\t  {\n316\t    \"name\": \"SDGD v2 on MAVEN (168 event types)\",\n317\t    \"description\": \"SDGD v2 (with merge-dedup) on MAVEN Event Detection. 168 event types partitioned into low-confusability batches via graph coloring. Compare vs single-pass, random batching v2, and per-type splitting v2.\\n\",\n318\t    \"results\": {\n319\t      \"single_pass\": {\n320\t        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.247,\n321\t        \"exact_f1\": 0.018,\n322\t        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.268,\n323\t        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.229,\n324\t        \"batches\": 1,\n325\t        \"time_s\": 82\n326\t      },\n327\t      \"sdgd_v2\": {\n328\t        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.282,\n329\t        \"exact_f1\": 0.028,\n330\t        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.258,\n331\t        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.312,\n332\t        \"batches\": 12,\n333\t        \"time_s\": 485,\n334\t        \"f1_delta_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+3.5\",\n335\t        \"precision_drop_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"-1.0\"\n336\t      },\n337\t      \"random_v2\": {\n338\t        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.265,\n339\t        \"exact_f1\": 0.022,\n340\t        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.251,\n341\t        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.281,\n342\t        \"batches\": 12,\n343\t        \"time_s\": 510\n344\t      },\n345\t      \"per_type_v2\": {\n346\t        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.291,\n347\t        \"exact_f1\": 0.031,\n348\t        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.261,\n349\t        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.329,\n350\t        \"batches\": 168,\n351\t        \"time_s\": 6250\n352\t      }\n353\t    },\n354\t    \"tables\": [\n355\t      {\n356\t        \"name\": \"MAVEN SDGD v2 Comparison\",\n357\t        \"columns\": [\n358\t          \"method\",\n359\t          \"relaxed_F1\",\n360\t          \"exact_F1\",\n361\t          \"precision\",\n362\t          \"recall\",\n363\t          \"batches\",\n364\t          \"time_s\"\n365\t        ],\n366\t        \"rows\": [\n367\t          [\n368\t            \"single_pass\",\n369\t            0.247,\n370\t            0.018,\n371\t            0.268,\n372\t            0.229,\n373\t            1,\n374\t            82\n375\t          ],\n376\t          [\n377\t            \"sdgd_v2\",\n378\t            0.282,\n379\t            0.028,\n380\t            0.258,\n381\t            0.312,\n382\t            12,\n383\t            485\n384\t          ],\n385\t          [\n386\t            \"random_v2\",\n387\t            0.265,\n388\t            0.022,\n389\t            0.251,\n390\t            0.281,\n391\t            12,\n392\t            510\n393\t          ],\n394\t          [\n395\t            \"per_type_v2\",\n396\t            0.291,\n397\t            0.031,\n398\t            0.261,\n399\t            0.329,\n400\t            168,\n401\t            6250\n402\t          ]\n403\t        ]\n404\t      }\n405\t    ],\n406\t    \"figures\": [],\n407\t    \"analysis\": \"MAVEN replication confirms SDGD v2 generalizes beyond NER. The key insight is that merge-dedup converts decomposition's recall boost into net F1 gain by controlling precision loss. On MAVEN specifically, the improvement is driven by better separation of FrameNet-sibling event types that single-pass conflates. Cross-task (NER+ED) SDGD v2 success strengthens Claim 3.\\n\\n\\nMAVEN's 168 event types create a dense confusability graph with many FrameNet-sibling clusters (e.g., \\\"Attack\\\" vs \\\"Catastrophe\\\" vs \\\"Destroying\\\"). SDGD v2's graph coloring separates these siblings across batches, reducing within-batch type confusion. The merge-dedup strategy is critical: SDGD-zero failed because multi-batch merging without dedup amplified false positives. Merge-dedup resolves this by (a) deduplicating overlapping trigger predictions across batches using span matching, and (b) selecting the highest-confidence prediction when conflicts arise. The +3.5pp relaxed F1 gain reflects genuine confusion reduction. The precision drop (-1.0pp) is within the ≤5pp budget because merge-dedup catches most spurious duplicates. The gain is larger than on Few-NERD (+3.5pp vs +3.5pp relaxed) in absolute terms because MAVEN's 168 types provide more room for decomposition: more confusable pairs to separate, and the single-pass baseline is more degraded. Random batching v2 captures partial benefit (batch reduction + dedup) but misses confusability-aware separation.\\n\"\n408\t  },\n409\t  {\n410\t    \"name\": \"SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (Empirical Calibration)\",\n411\t    \"description\": \"SDGD-cal v2: confusability graph from 5-10 calibration examples (empirical confusion matrix) instead of embedding SCS. Compare vs SDGD-zero v2 and single-pass on Few-NERD.\\n\",\n412\t    \"results\": {\n413\t      \"sdgd_cal_v2\": {\n414\t        \"f1\": 0.493,\n415\t        \"precision\": 0.548,\n416\t        \"recall\": 0.449,\n417\t        \"batches\": 8,\n418\t        \"time_s\": 405,\n419\t        \"calibration_calls\": 660,\n420\t        \"calibration_time_s\": 320\n421\t      },\n422\t      \"sdgd_zero_v2\": {\n423\t        \"f1\": 0.48,\n424\t        \"precision\": 0.538,\n425\t        \"recall\": 0.433\n426\t      },\n427\t      \"single_pass\": {\n428\t        \"f1\": 0.445,\n429\t        \"precision\": 0.563,\n430\t        \"recall\": 0.368\n431\t      },\n432\t      \"deltas\": {\n433\t        \"sdgd_cal_vs_zero_pp\": \"+1.3\",\n434\t        \"sdgd_cal_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+4.8\"\n435\t      },\n436\t      \"calibration_vs_embedding\": {\n437\t        \"spearman_rho_scs_vs_empirical\": 0.42,\n438\t        \"kendall_tau_scs_vs_empirical\": 0.3,\n439\t        \"n_pair_rank_disagreements_top50\": 11\n440\t      }\n441\t    },\n442\t    \"tables\": [\n443\t      {\n444\t        \"name\": \"Few-NERD SDGD-cal vs SDGD-zero\",\n445\t        \"columns\": [\n446\t          \"method\",\n447\t          \"F1\",\n448\t          \"precision\",\n449\t          \"recall\",\n450\t          \"Δ_vs_single\"\n451\t        ],\n452\t        \"rows\": [\n453\t          [\n454\t            \"single_pass\",\n455\t            0.445,\n456\t            0.563,\n457\t            0.368,\n458\t            \"—\"\n459\t          ],\n460\t          [\n461\t            \"sdgd_zero_v2\",\n462\t            0.48,\n463\t            0.538,\n464\t            0.433,\n465\t            \"+3.5pp\"\n466\t          ],\n467\t          [\n468\t            \"sdgd_cal_v2\",\n469\t            0.493,\n470\t            0.548,\n471\t            0.449,\n472\t            \"+4.8pp\"\n473\t          ]\n474\t        ]\n475\t      }\n476\t    ],\n477\t    \"figures\": [],\n478\t    \"analysis\": \"SDGD-cal establishes an upper bound for embedding-free SDGD. The ρ=0.42 correlation between SCS and empirical confusion validates the embedding proxy while showing room for improvement. In practice, SDGD-cal is recommended when calibration data is available; SDGD-zero when it is not. Both outperform single-pass, confirming Claim 3.\\n\\n\\nEmpirical calibration measures actual model confusion on held-out examples, capturing model-specific biases that embedding SCS approximates but cannot fully predict. For example, Qwen3 may confuse \\\"building-library\\\" ↔ \\\"building-other\\\" more than their embedding similarity suggests (due to prompt formatting or training distribution), and conversely may handle \\\"person-politician\\\" ↔ \\\"person-scholar\\\" better than expected. These rank disagreements (11/50 in top-50 pairs) are enough to reshuffle the confusability graph and produce marginally better batch assignments. The +1.3pp gain over SDGD-zero is modest because (a) the embedding proxy is already ρ=0.42 correlated with empirical confusion, capturing the dominant similarity axis, and (b) Few-NERD's cross-domain schema has clear cluster boundaries that both methods identify. The calibration overhead (660 API calls, ~5min) is acceptable for offline schema decomposition planning. The main value of SDGD-cal is that it bypasses embedding choice entirely — critical for practitioners who cannot determine which embedding best suits their schema a priori.\\n\"\n479\t  },\n480\t  {\n481\t    \"name\": \"SDGD-cal on MAVEN (Empirical Calibration, 168 types)\",\n482\t    \"description\": \"SDGD-cal v2 on MAVEN with empirical confusion matrix from calibration examples. Full comparison table: single-pass / SDGD-zero v2 / SDGD-cal v2 / random v2 / per-type v2.\\n\",\n483\t    \"results\": {\n484\t      \"sdgd_cal_v2\": {\n485\t        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.298,\n486\t        \"exact_f1\": 0.032,\n487\t        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.264,\n488\t        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.342,\n489\t        \"batches\": 12,\n490\t        \"calibration_calls\": 1680,\n491\t        \"calibration_time_s\": 810\n492\t      },\n493\t      \"sdgd_zero_v2\": {\n494\t        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.282,\n495\t        \"exact_f1\": 0.028\n496\t      },\n497\t      \"single_pass\": {\n498\t        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.247,\n499\t        \"exact_f1\": 0.018\n500\t      },\n501\t      \"deltas\": {\n502\t        \"sdgd_cal_vs_zero_pp\": \"+1.6\",\n503\t        \"sdgd_cal_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+5.1\"\n504\t      }\n505\t    },\n506\t    \"tables\": [\n507\t      {\n508\t        \"name\": \"MAVEN Full Comparison Table\",\n509\t        \"columns\": [\n510\t          \"method\",\n511\t          \"relaxed_F1\",\n512\t          \"exact_F1\",\n513\t          \"batches\",\n514\t          \"total_time_s\"\n515\t        ],\n516\t        \"rows\": [\n517\t          [\n518\t            \"single_pass\",\n519\t            0.247,\n520\t            0.018,\n521\t            1,\n522\t            82\n523\t          ],\n524\t          [\n525\t            \"random_v2\",\n526\t            0.265,\n527\t            0.022,\n528\t            12,\n529\t            510\n530\t          ],\n531\t          [\n532\t            \"sdgd_zero_v2\",\n533\t            0.282,\n534\t            0.028,\n535\t            12,\n536\t            485\n537\t          ],\n538\t          [\n539\t            \"sdgd_cal_v2\",\n540\t            0.298,\n541\t            0.032,\n542\t            12,\n543\t            1295\n544\t          ],\n545\t          [\n546\t            \"per_type_v2\",\n547\t            0.291,\n548\t            0.031,\n549\t            168,\n550\t            6250\n551\t          ]\n552\t        ]\n553\t      }\n554\t    ],\n555\t    \"figures\": [],\n556\t    \"analysis\": \"Notably, SDGD-cal v2 (0.298) slightly exceeds per-type v2 (0.291) on MAVEN. This is because per-type splitting eliminates within-batch confusion but loses ALL cross-type context, while SDGD-cal retains beneficial cross-type context (non-confusable types in the same batch help the model discriminate). This inverted result — SDGD-cal > per-type — is a strong argument for confusability-AWARE decomposition over naive per-type splitting. It also addresses the UniNER comparison directly.\\n\\n\\nSDGD-cal's advantage over SDGD-zero is slightly larger on MAVEN (+1.6pp vs +1.3pp on Few-NERD) because MAVEN's homogeneous FrameNet-based schema makes embedding similarity less discriminative. Many event type pairs have uniformly high SCS (mean=0.216, narrow distribution), so the embedding proxy struggles to rank confusability among mid-range pairs. Empirical calibration captures within-cluster confusion structure that embedding cosine similarity compresses: \\\"Attack\\\" and \\\"Catastrophe\\\" may have similar SCS to \\\"Attack\\\" and \\\"Protest\\\", but the actual confusion rates differ substantially due to trigger word overlap patterns. The 1680 calibration calls (168 types × 10 examples) are the main cost, but acceptable for a one-time schema analysis. The +5.1pp gain over single-pass on MAVEN is the largest SDGD improvement across all experiments, reflecting MAVEN's 168-type schema as the highest-complexity setting.\\n\"\n557\t  },\n558\t  {\n559\t    \"name\": \"LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation\",\n560\t    \"description\": \"Full replication of exp-002 protocol with LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct on Few-NERD. Count curve + BCS-controlled batches + pairwise analysis + per-type analysis.\\n\",\n561\t    \"results\": {\n562\t      \"count_curve\": {\n563\t        \"count_1\": {\n564\t          \"f1\": 0.351,\n565\t          \"precision\": 0.248,\n566\t          \"recall\": 0.598\n567\t        },\n568\t        \"count_6\": {\n569\t          \"f1\": 0.328,\n570\t          \"precision\": 0.258,\n571\t          \"recall\": 0.451\n572\t        },\n573\t        \"count_12\": {\n574\t          \"f1\": 0.364,\n575\t          \"precision\": 0.396,\n576\t          \"recall\": 0.337\n577\t        },\n578\t        \"count_22\": {\n579\t          \"f1\": 0.318,\n580\t          \"precision\": 0.372,\n581\t          \"recall\": 0.278\n582\t        },\n583\t        \"count_44\": {\n584\t          \"f1\": 0.432,\n585\t          \"precision\": 0.51,\n586\t          \"recall\": 0.375\n587\t        },\n588\t        \"count_64\": {\n589\t          \"f1\": 0.408,\n590\t          \"precision\": 0.625,\n591\t          \"recall\": 0.302\n592\t        }\n593\t      },\n594\t      \"pairwise_analysis\": {\n595\t        \"all_pairs\": {\n596\t          \"n_pairs\": 1953,\n597\t          \"pearson_r\": 0.138,\n598\t          \"pearson_p\": 2.1e-09,\n599\t          \"spearman_rho\": 0.142,\n600\t          \"spearman_p\": 8.4e-10\n601\t        },\n602\t        \"nonzero_only\": {\n603\t          \"spearman_rho\": 0.221,\n604\t          \"spearman_p\": 3.5e-05\n605\t        },\n606\t        \"count_12\": {\n607\t          \"pearson_r\": 0.129,\n608\t          \"pearson_p\": 1.8e-08\n609\t        }\n610\t      },\n611\t      \"extreme_batch_confusion\": {\n612\t        \"count_6\": {\n613\t          \"high_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.058,\n614\t          \"low_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.003,\n615\t          \"ratio\": 19.3\n616\t        },\n617\t        \"count_12\": {\n618\t          \"high_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.041,\n619\t          \"low_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.005,\n620\t          \"ratio\": 8.2\n621\t        }\n622\t      },\n623\t      \"bcs_controlled\": {\n624\t        \"count_6_low_bcs_f1\": 0.358,\n625\t        \"count_6_high_bcs_f1\": 0.271,\n626\t        \"count_6_delta_pp\": -8.7,\n627\t        \"count_12_low_bcs_f1\": 0.391,\n628\t        \"count_12_high_bcs_f1\": 0.286,\n629\t        \"count_12_delta_pp\": -10.5\n630\t      },\n631\t      \"per_type_analysis\": {\n632\t        \"n_types\": 63,\n633\t        \"spearman_rho\": -0.248,\n634\t        \"spearman_p\": 0.049,\n635\t        \"partial_r_ctrl_logfreq\": -0.172,\n636\t        \"partial_p\": 0.178\n637\t      },\n638\t      \"cross_model_comparison\": {\n639\t        \"qwen3_pairwise_r\": 0.156,\n640\t        \"llama_pairwise_r\": 0.138,\n641\t        \"qwen3_confusion_ratio_range\": \"10-28x\",\n642\t        \"llama_confusion_ratio_range\": \"8-19x\",\n643\t        \"qwen3_bcs_delta_count12\": -10.1,\n644\t        \"llama_bcs_delta_count12\": -10.5\n645\t      }\n646\t    },\n647\t    \"tables\": [\n648\t      {\n649\t        \"name\": \"Cross-Model Pairwise Comparison\",\n650\t        \"columns\": [\n651\t          \"metric\",\n652\t          \"Qwen3-32B\",\n653\t          \"LLaMA-70B\"\n654\t        ],\n655\t        \"rows\": [\n656\t          [\n657\t            \"pairwise Pearson r\",\n658\t            0.156,\n659\t            0.138\n660\t          ],\n661\t          [\n662\t            \"pairwise p-value\",\n663\t            \"4.6e-12\",\n664\t            \"2.1e-09\"\n665\t          ],\n666\t          [\n667\t            \"nonzero Spearman ρ\",\n668\t            0.254,\n669\t            0.221\n670\t          ],\n671\t          [\n672\t            \"confusion ratio (count_6 extreme)\",\n673\t            \"27.5x\",\n674\t            \"19.3x\"\n675\t          ],\n676\t          [\n677\t            \"confusion ratio (count_12 extreme)\",\n678\t            \"9.9x\",\n679\t            \"8.2x\"\n680\t          ],\n681\t          [\n682\t            \"BCS-controlled Δ (count_12)\",\n683\t            \"-10.1pp\",\n684\t            \"-10.5pp\"\n685\t          ],\n686\t          [\n687\t            \"per-type ρ\",\n688\t            -0.293,\n689\t            -0.248\n690\t          ]\n691\t        ]\n692\t      },\n693\t      {\n694\t        \"name\": \"LLaMA Count Curve\",\n695\t        \"columns\": [\n696\t          \"count\",\n697\t          \"F1\",\n698\t          \"precision\",\n699\t          \"recall\"\n700\t        ],\n701\t        \"rows\": [\n702\t          [\n703\t            1,\n704\t            0.351,\n705\t            0.248,\n706\t            0.598\n707\t          ],\n708\t          [\n709\t            6,\n710\t            0.328,\n711\t            0.258,\n712\t            0.451\n713\t          ],\n714\t          [\n715\t            12,\n716\t            0.364,\n717\t            0.396,\n718\t            0.337\n719\t          ],\n720\t          [\n721\t            22,\n722\t            0.318,\n723\t            0.372,\n724\t            0.278\n725\t          ],\n726\t          [\n727\t            44,\n728\t            0.432,\n729\t            0.51,\n730\t            0.375\n731\t          ],\n732\t          [\n733\t            64,\n734\t            0.408,\n735\t            0.625,\n736\t            0.302\n737\t          ]\n738\t        ]\n739\t      }\n740\t    ],\n741\t    \"figures\": [],\n742\t    \"analysis\": \"Cross-model validation is the single most important experiment for reviewer concern mitigation. Both models agree on direction, significance, and approximate magnitude of the pairwise confusability effect. The residual differences (r=0.156 vs 0.138, ratio 28x vs 19x) are consistent with model capacity differences rather than contradictory findings. This eliminates the \\\"model-specific artifact\\\" concern and allows the paper to claim the confusability signal is a property of the task structure, not the model.\\n\\n\\nLLaMA-3.3-70B exhibits the same pairwise confusability signal as Qwen3-32B because the underlying mechanism — semantically similar co-present types cause type interference during label assignment — is architecture-independent. Both models are decoder-only transformers that process type labels as natural language tokens; when two labels share semantic content, the model's next-token prediction distributes probability mass across both, increasing confusion. The effect size is slightly attenuated (r=0.138 vs 0.156) because LLaMA-70B's larger parameter count provides marginally better type discrimination capacity. The confusion ratio (8-19x) is in the same order as Qwen3 (10-28x) but lower because the 70B model makes fewer type confusion errors per entity overall. The per-type signal is weaker (ρ=-0.248, borderline p=0.049) because per-type aggregation introduces noise and LLaMA's different tokenization creates slight rank perturbations. Critically, the pairwise correlation (r=0.138, p=2.1e-9) is highly significant and falls within the best-tier range [0.10, 0.20], confirming the signal is not model-specific. The BCS-controlled delta (-10.5pp at count_12) falls within the cross-model range [-35, -15]pp when scaled to larger schemas.\\n\"\n743\t  },\n744\t  {\n745\t    \"name\": \"Random Batching Ablation (Same k as SDGD)\",\n746\t    \"description\": \"SDGD v2 vs random batching v2 (same batch count k=8) on Few-NERD. Both use merge-dedup. Isolates confusability-aware grouping from batch count reduction.\\n\",\n747\t    \"results\": {\n748\t      \"sdgd_v2\": {\n749\t        \"f1\": 0.48,\n750\t        \"precision\": 0.538,\n751\t        \"recall\": 0.433,\n752\t        \"batches\": 8,\n753\t        \"time_s\": 395,\n754\t        \"std_across_5_seeds\": 0.008\n755\t      },\n756\t      \"random_v2\": {\n757\t        \"f1\": 0.458,\n758\t        \"precision\": 0.521,\n759\t        \"recall\": 0.411,\n760\t        \"batches\": 8,\n761\t        \"time_s\": 410,\n762\t        \"std_across_5_seeds\": 0.015\n763\t      },\n764\t      \"single_pass\": {\n765\t        \"f1\": 0.445,\n766\t        \"precision\": 0.563,\n767\t        \"recall\": 0.368\n768\t      },\n769\t      \"deltas\": {\n770\t        \"sdgd_v2_vs_random_v2_pp\": \"+2.2\",\n771\t        \"random_v2_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+1.3\",\n772\t        \"sdgd_v2_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+3.5\"\n773\t      },\n774\t      \"decomposition_attribution\": {\n775\t        \"batch_reduction_contribution_pp\": 1.3,\n776\t        \"confusability_aware_grouping_pp\": 2.2,\n777\t        \"total_sdgd_gain_pp\": 3.5,\n778\t        \"pct_from_grouping\": 62.9\n779\t      }\n780\t    },\n781\t    \"tables\": [\n782\t      {\n783\t        \"name\": \"Ablation: Grouping vs Batch Reduction\",\n784\t        \"columns\": [\n785\t          \"method\",\n786\t          \"F1\",\n787\t          \"Δ_vs_single\",\n788\t          \"σ(5 seeds)\",\n789\t          \"source_of_gain\"\n790\t        ],\n791\t        \"rows\": [\n792\t          [\n793\t            \"single_pass\",\n794\t            0.445,\n795\t            \"—\",\n796\t            \"—\",\n797\t            \"—\"\n798\t          ],\n799\t          [\n800\t            \"random_v2 (k=8)\",\n801\t            0.458,\n802\t            \"+1.3pp\",\n803\t            0.015,\n804\t            \"batch reduction only\"\n805\t          ],\n806\t          [\n807\t            \"sdgd_v2 (k=8)\",\n808\t            0.48,\n809\t            \"+3.5pp\",\n810\t            0.008,\n811\t            \"batch reduction + grouping\"\n812\t          ]\n813\t        ]\n814\t      }\n815\t    ],\n816\t    \"figures\": [],\n817\t    \"analysis\": \"This ablation is essential for Claim 3: it proves SDGD's value is primarily from confusability-aware grouping (63%), not mere batch reduction. Without this ablation, a reviewer could argue that any decomposition strategy would work equally well. The 2.2pp gap, combined with SDGD's lower variance, makes a clean case for principled decomposition.\\n\\n\\nMerge-dedup resolves the core precision problem for ALL decomposition strategies, so both random and SDGD benefit equally from dedup. The remaining difference (2.2pp) isolates the value of confusability-aware batch composition. Random batching gets 1.3pp from batch reduction alone (smaller batches → better per-entity attention → higher recall, with dedup controlling precision). SDGD adds 2.2pp by ensuring confusable type pairs are separated, reducing within-batch type confusion. The 62.9% attribution to grouping (vs 37.1% to batch reduction) quantifies SDGD's specific contribution. SDGD also shows lower variance (σ=0.008 vs 0.015) because its batch composition is deterministic and optimized, while random batching quality fluctuates depending on which confusable types happen to co-occur. The lower variance is practically important — SDGD gives predictable quality.\\n\"\n818\t  },\n819\t  {\n820\t    \"name\": \"Per-Type Splitting Ablation (UniNER-style)\",\n821\t    \"description\": \"Per-type splitting v2 (1 type per batch, k=66) on Few-NERD with merge-dedup. Upper bound on decomposition benefit. Quality-latency tradeoff vs SDGD v2.\\n\",\n822\t    \"results\": {\n823\t      \"per_type_v2\": {\n824\t        \"f1\": 0.492,\n825\t        \"precision\": 0.545,\n826\t        \"recall\": 0.449,\n827\t        \"batches\": 66,\n828\t        \"time_s\": 2480,\n829\t        \"api_calls\": 66\n830\t      },\n831\t      \"sdgd_v2\": {\n832\t        \"f1\": 0.48,\n833\t        \"precision\": 0.538,\n834\t        \"recall\": 0.433,\n835\t        \"batches\": 8,\n836\t        \"time_s\": 395,\n837\t        \"api_calls\": 8\n838\t      },\n839\t      \"single_pass\": {\n840\t        \"f1\": 0.445,\n841\t        \"precision\": 0.563,\n842\t        \"recall\": 0.368,\n843\t        \"batches\": 1,\n844\t        \"time_s\": 78,\n845\t        \"api_calls\": 1\n846\t      },\n847\t      \"quality_vs_latency\": {\n848\t        \"sdgd_v2_quality_pct_of_per_type\": 97.6,\n849\t        \"sdgd_v2_latency_pct_of_per_type\": 15.9,\n850\t        \"sdgd_v2_api_call_pct_of_per_type\": 12.1\n851\t      },\n852\t      \"per_type_vs_sdgd_delta_pp\": \"+1.2\",\n853\t      \"residual_confusion_analysis\": {\n854\t        \"sdgd_residual_confusion_pairs\": 23,\n855\t        \"mean_scs_of_residual\": 0.612,\n856\t        \"max_scs_of_residual\": 0.743\n857\t      }\n858\t    },\n859\t    \"tables\": [\n860\t      {\n861\t        \"name\": \"Quality-Latency Pareto Frontier\",\n862\t        \"columns\": [\n863\t          \"method\",\n864\t          \"F1\",\n865\t          \"batches\",\n866\t          \"time_s\",\n867\t          \"F1_per_minute\"\n868\t        ],\n869\t        \"rows\": [\n870\t          [\n871\t            \"single_pass\",\n872\t            0.445,\n873\t            1,\n874\t            78,\n875\t            0.342\n876\t          ],\n877\t          [\n878\t            \"sdgd_v2\",\n879\t            0.48,\n880\t            8,\n881\t            395,\n882\t            0.073\n883\t          ],\n884\t          [\n885\t            \"per_type_v2\",\n886\t            0.492,\n887\t            66,\n888\t            2480,\n889\t            0.012\n890\t          ]\n891\t        ]\n892\t      }\n893\t    ],\n894\t    \"figures\": [],\n895\t    \"analysis\": \"The per-type ablation addresses the UniNER comparison directly. UniNER-style per-type splitting provides the highest quality but at prohibitive cost for real-time applications. SDGD v2 offers a principled middle ground — it captures 97.6% of the quality with a data-driven criterion for WHICH types need separation. This is the operational contribution: not just \\\"decompose the schema\\\" but \\\"decompose it optimally given a latency budget.\\\"\\n\\n\\nPer-type splitting eliminates within-batch type confusion entirely — with one type per batch, there is no co-present type to confuse with. Merge-dedup handles cross-batch deduplication. The resulting F1 (0.492) is the theoretical ceiling for decomposition-based approaches. SDGD v2 achieves 97.6% of this ceiling at 15.9% of the latency because graph coloring concentrates separation effort on the most confusable pairs (SCS > 0.8), while allowing low-similarity types to share batches safely. The residual 1.2pp gap comes from 23 moderately confusable pairs (mean SCS=0.612) that SDGD groups together to keep k=8. These pairs cause occasional type confusion that per-type avoids entirely. The tradeoff is clear: 1.2pp F1 costs 6.3x latency and 8.25x API calls. For production deployment, SDGD v2 dominates per-type splitting on cost-effectiveness. For offline analysis where latency is irrelevant, per-type v2 provides the best quality.\\n\"\n896\t  },\n897\t  {\n898\t    \"name\": \"SCS Proxy Validation (Embedding vs Empirical Confusion)\",\n899\t    \"description\": \"Rank correlation between embedding SCS and empirical confusion matrix. Validates embedding proxy for SDGD-zero practical use.\\n\",\n900\t    \"results\": {\n901\t      \"fewnerd_qwen3\": {\n902\t        \"spearman_rho\": 0.43,\n903\t        \"kendall_tau\": 0.31,\n904\t        \"n_pairs_with_confusion\": 287,\n905\t        \"top10_overlap\": 7\n906\t      },\n907\t      \"maven_minilm\": {\n908\t        \"spearman_rho\": 0.38,\n909\t        \"kendall_tau\": 0.27,\n910\t        \"n_pairs_with_confusion\": 1842,\n911\t        \"top10_overlap\": 6\n912\t      },\n913\t      \"maven_qwen3\": {\n914\t        \"spearman_rho\": 0.15,\n915\t        \"kendall_tau\": 0.11,\n916\t        \"n_pairs_with_confusion\": 1842,\n917\t        \"top10_overlap\": 2\n918\t      },\n919\t      \"fewnerd_minilm\": {\n920\t        \"spearman_rho\": 0.22,\n921\t        \"kendall_tau\": 0.16,\n922\t        \"n_pairs_with_confusion\": 287,\n923\t        \"top10_overlap\": 4\n924\t      },\n925\t      \"cross_validation\": {\n926\t        \"best_embedding_per_dataset\": {\n927\t          \"fewnerd\": \"Qwen3 (ρ=0.43)\",\n928\t          \"maven\": \"MiniLM (ρ=0.38)\"\n929\t        },\n930\t        \"pattern\": \"extraction-model embeddings excel on cross-domain schemas; generic embeddings excel on homogeneous schemas\"\n931\t      }\n932\t    },\n933\t    \"tables\": [\n934\t      {\n935\t        \"name\": \"SCS Proxy Validation Matrix\",\n936\t        \"columns\": [\n937\t          \"dataset\",\n938\t          \"embedding\",\n939\t          \"Spearman_ρ\",\n940\t          \"Kendall_τ\",\n941\t          \"top10_overlap\"\n942\t        ],\n943\t        \"rows\": [\n944\t          [\n945\t            \"Few-NERD\",\n946\t            \"Qwen3\",\n947\t            0.43,\n948\t            0.31,\n949\t            \"7/10\"\n950\t          ],\n951\t          [\n952\t            \"Few-NERD\",\n953\t            \"MiniLM\",\n954\t            0.22,\n955\t            0.16,\n956\t            \"4/10\"\n957\t          ],\n958\t          [\n959\t            \"MAVEN\",\n960\t            \"MiniLM\",\n961\t            0.38,\n962\t            0.27,\n963\t            \"6/10\"\n964\t          ],\n965\t          [\n966\t            \"MAVEN\",\n967\t            \"Qwen3\",\n968\t            0.15,\n969\t            0.11,\n970\t            \"2/10\"\n971\t          ]\n972\t        ]\n973\t      }\n974\t    ],\n975\t    \"figures\": [],\n976\t    \"analysis\": \"Validates SDGD-zero as a practical no-calibration method while explaining why SDGD-cal improves: the ρ=0.43 ceiling for embedding-based proxy means ~18% of variance in confusion is captured, leaving room for empirical calibration. The cross-embedding pattern provides practical guidance: use extraction-model embeddings for diverse schemas, generic embeddings for domain-specific schemas, or SDGD-cal to bypass the choice entirely. This completes the SDGD framework: SDGD-zero for zero-cost deployment, SDGD-cal for maximum quality.\\n\\nThe SCS-confusion correlation (ρ=0.38-0.43 for best embedding per dataset) validates the embedding proxy as a reasonable but imperfect predictor of actual model confusion. The imperfection is expected: embedding SCS captures semantic similarity (the dominant confusion axis) but not model-specific factors like tokenization artifacts, prompt position effects, or label frequency biases. The 2×2 pattern (Qwen3 better for Few-NERD, MiniLM better for MAVEN) replicates the D010 finding at the validated pairwise level. For Few-NERD's cross-domain schema, Qwen3 embeddings align with the extraction model's internal representations, capturing model-specific confusability. For MAVEN's homogeneous FrameNet schema, Qwen3 compresses within-domain distinctions while MiniLM's generic sentence embedding preserves them. The top-10 overlap (6-7/10) shows that both best-embedding conditions identify the most confusable pairs with reasonable fidelity, validating SDGD-zero's practical utility. The gap between ρ=0.43 and a hypothetical perfect proxy (ρ=1.0) explains SDGD-cal's incremental gain.\\n\"\n977\t  }\n978\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa022cab-8de9-4dd8-8336-28674242c52a","uuid":"442fe018-d249-47c2-8e0d-ba6d1cc30667","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:24:40.775Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T140829/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"DocRED Cross-Task Replication (96 relations)\",\n    \"description\": \"Count curve + within-count BCS correlation on DocRED document-level RE (96 relation types). Scaling stress test beyond sentence-level FewRel.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"count_curve\": {\n        \"count_1_acc\": 0.782,\n        \"count_4_acc\": 0.753,\n        \"count_8_acc\": 0.684,\n        \"count_16_acc\": 0.591,\n        \"count_32_acc\": 0.502,\n        \"count_64_acc\": 0.421,\n        \"count_96_acc\": 0.358,\n        \"degradation_total_pp\": -42.4\n      },\n      \"bcs_controlled\": {\n        \"count_8_low_bcs_acc\": 0.745,\n        \"count_8_high_bcs_acc\": 0.462,\n        \"count_8_delta_pp\": -28.3,\n        \"count_16_low_bcs_acc\": 0.718,\n        \"count_16_high_bcs_acc\": 0.431,\n        \"count_16_delta_pp\": -28.7\n      },\n      \"pairwise\": {\n        \"n_pairs\": 4560,\n        \"pearson_r\": 0.131,\n        \"pearson_p\": 3.8e-09\n      },\n      \"vs_fewrel_80rel\": {\n        \"degradation_pp\": \"-42.4 vs -35.3\",\n        \"bcs_effect_pp\": \"-28.5 vs -31.2\",\n        \"amplification_vs_16rel\": \"~4.1x\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"DocRED Count Curve\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"count\",\n          \"accuracy\",\n          \"bcs_mean\",\n          \"n_batches\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            0.782,\n            0.0,\n            96\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            0.753,\n            0.618,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            8,\n            0.684,\n            0.702,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            16,\n            0.591,\n            0.781,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            32,\n            0.502,\n            0.874,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            64,\n            0.421,\n            0.938,\n            5\n          ],\n          [\n            96,\n            0.358,\n            0.967,\n            5\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"DocRED BCS-Controlled Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"count\",\n          \"low_bcs_acc\",\n          \"high_bcs_acc\",\n          \"delta_pp\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            8,\n            0.745,\n            0.462,\n            -28.3\n          ],\n          [\n            16,\n            0.718,\n            0.431,\n            -28.7\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Third RE dataset confirms confusability signal on document-level extraction. BCS effect amplification from FewRel-16 (9.1pp) → FewRel-80 (31.2pp) → DocRED-96 (28.5pp) shows the effect scales with schema complexity, with saturation above ~80 types. Pairwise r=0.131 provides 4th independent replication of pairwise confusability signal across tasks and schema sizes.\\n\\n\\nDocument-level RE introduces cross-sentence reasoning and coreference complexity, lowering baseline accuracy (~78% vs FewRel's 86%). But the confusability mechanism operates at the type-assignment stage regardless of extraction granularity. DocRED's 96 relation types include many fine-grained distinctions (\\\"place of birth\\\" vs \\\"place of death\\\", \\\"member of\\\" vs \\\"part of\\\") creating dense high-confusability clusters. The total degradation (42.4pp) exceeds FewRel's 35.3pp because the document-level context amplifies attention dilution (Channel 1) while the denser relation taxonomy amplifies type confusion (Channel 2). The BCS effect (-28.5pp) is comparable to FewRel because confusability operates at the same mechanism; the slight reduction reflects the fact that some DocRED confusion is driven by discourse structure rather than type semantics alone. Pairwise r=0.131 replicates the range seen on Few-NERD (0.156) and MAVEN-MiniLM (0.120), confirming the signal generalizes to document-level RE.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"2×2 Interaction Effect (Count × Confusability)\",\n    \"description\": \"Factorial design: {low_count=6, high_count=22} × {low_BCS, high_BCS} on Few-NERD. Tests whether count and confusability contribute additively or interact.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"cell_means\": {\n        \"low_count_low_bcs_f1\": 0.372,\n        \"low_count_high_bcs_f1\": 0.288,\n        \"high_count_low_bcs_f1\": 0.318,\n        \"high_count_high_bcs_f1\": 0.228\n      },\n      \"main_effects\": {\n        \"count_effect_pp\": -5.7,\n        \"bcs_effect_pp\": -8.7,\n        \"combined_observed_pp\": -14.4,\n        \"sum_of_individual_pp\": -14.4\n      },\n      \"anova\": {\n        \"count_F\": 8.42,\n        \"count_p\": 0.006,\n        \"bcs_F\": 14.73,\n        \"bcs_p\": 0.001,\n        \"interaction_F\": 0.31,\n        \"interaction_p\": 0.42,\n        \"model_R2\": 0.74\n      },\n      \"error_channel_decomposition\": {\n        \"low_count_low_bcs_recall\": 0.482,\n        \"low_count_high_bcs_recall\": 0.464,\n        \"high_count_low_bcs_recall\": 0.385,\n        \"high_count_high_bcs_recall\": 0.362,\n        \"recall_delta_by_count\": -0.099,\n        \"recall_delta_by_bcs\": -0.021,\n        \"low_count_low_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.008,\n        \"low_count_high_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.042,\n        \"high_count_low_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.012,\n        \"high_count_high_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.048,\n        \"confusion_delta_by_count\": 0.005,\n        \"confusion_delta_by_bcs\": 0.035\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"2×2 Factorial Results (F1)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"\",\n          \"low_BCS\",\n          \"high_BCS\",\n          \"BCS_effect\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"count=6\",\n            0.372,\n            0.288,\n            \"-8.4pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"count=22\",\n            0.318,\n            0.228,\n            \"-9.0pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"count_effect\",\n            \"-5.4pp\",\n            \"-6.0pp\",\n            \"\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"ANOVA Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"source\",\n          \"F\",\n          \"p\",\n          \"eta_sq\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"count\",\n            8.42,\n            0.006,\n            0.18\n          ],\n          [\n            \"BCS\",\n            14.73,\n            0.001,\n            0.32\n          ],\n          [\n            \"count×BCS\",\n            0.31,\n            0.42,\n            0.01\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Non-significant interaction is the key result — directly validates the orthogonal channel model central to Claim 2. The error decomposition shows clean separation: count primarily drives recall loss (9.9pp vs 2.1pp BCS effect on recall), while BCS primarily drives type confusion (3.5x vs 1.4x count effect on confusion rate). This mechanistic dissociation is the strongest evidence for the multi-channel degradation thesis.\\n\\n\\nThe orthogonal channel model predicts additive effects because count and confusability degrade extraction at different processing stages. Count→recall operates at the entity detection stage: more types in the prompt dilute attention, causing entities to be missed entirely. The recall penalty (~10pp from count=6 to count=22) is nearly constant across BCS levels because attention dilution depends on prompt length, not type similarity. Confusability→type confusion operates at the type assignment stage: semantically similar co-present types interfere during label selection. The confusion rate penalty (~3.5x from low to high BCS) is nearly constant across count levels because type interference depends on pairwise similarity, not total count. The non-significant interaction (F=0.31, p=0.42) confirms these are independent channels. The combined penalty (14.4pp) equals the sum of individual penalties (5.7+8.7=14.4), ruling out multiplicative interaction. R²=0.74 indicates the two-factor additive model explains most of the variance in extraction quality.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Systematic BCS-Binned Error Taxonomy\",\n    \"description\": \"Systematic FP classification (intra-batch type substitution / extra-schema substitution / boundary error / hallucination) binned by BCS quartiles. Extends partial analysis from reeval_text_match to full BCS gradient.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bcs_bin_zero\": {\n        \"n_fp\": 6172,\n        \"intra_sub_pct\": 0.0,\n        \"extra_sub_pct\": 90.3,\n        \"boundary_pct\": 6.8,\n        \"hallucination_pct\": 2.9\n      },\n      \"bcs_bin_low\": {\n        \"n_fp\": 2231,\n        \"intra_sub_pct\": 1.6,\n        \"extra_sub_pct\": 89.9,\n        \"boundary_pct\": 5.7,\n        \"hallucination_pct\": 2.8\n      },\n      \"bcs_bin_medium\": {\n        \"n_fp\": 3843,\n        \"intra_sub_pct\": 10.6,\n        \"extra_sub_pct\": 78.1,\n        \"boundary_pct\": 7.2,\n        \"hallucination_pct\": 4.1\n      },\n      \"bcs_bin_high\": {\n        \"n_fp\": 6188,\n        \"intra_sub_pct\": 49.4,\n        \"extra_sub_pct\": 30.8,\n        \"boundary_pct\": 14.2,\n        \"hallucination_pct\": 5.6\n      },\n      \"correlations\": {\n        \"intra_sub_vs_bcs_spearman_rho\": 1.0,\n        \"intra_sub_vs_bcs_p\": \"<0.001\",\n        \"chi_square\": 6952,\n        \"chi_square_p\": \"~0\"\n      },\n      \"per_100_gold\": {\n        \"bcs_zero_intra_sub\": 0.0,\n        \"bcs_low_intra_sub\": 3.4,\n        \"bcs_medium_intra_sub\": 6.47,\n        \"bcs_high_intra_sub\": 12.93\n      },\n      \"exp2_controlled_trend\": \"IntraSub rate 3.2% → 7.3% → 8.6% (rho=1.0, p<0.001)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Error Type Distribution by BCS Bin\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"BCS_bin\",\n          \"n_FP\",\n          \"intra_sub_%\",\n          \"extra_sub_%\",\n          \"boundary_%\",\n          \"halluc_%\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0.00\",\n            6172,\n            0.0,\n            90.3,\n            6.8,\n            2.9\n          ],\n          [\n            \"<0.85\",\n            2231,\n            1.6,\n            89.9,\n            5.7,\n            2.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.85-0.95\",\n            3843,\n            10.6,\n            78.1,\n            7.2,\n            4.1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"≥0.95\",\n            6188,\n            49.4,\n            30.8,\n            14.2,\n            5.6\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"These results (largely already in registry) are the mechanistic backbone of Claim 2. The near-perfect monotonic relationship (ρ=1.0) between BCS and type confusion proportion is the most direct evidence that semantic similarity causes type confusion, not merely correlates with it. The error taxonomy also rules out alternative explanations: hallucination and boundary errors are BCS-independent, confirming confusability operates specifically on the type discrimination channel.\\n\\n\\nRegistry already contains core results (exp007_error_taxonomy). Under best tier, the systematic BCS-binned analysis confirms a clean monotonic gradient: intra-batch type substitution dominates FP at high BCS (49.4%) while being nearly absent at BCS=0. The transition is smooth — BCS acts as a continuous predictor of error type composition, not a threshold effect. Boundary errors and hallucinations remain low (<15%) and BCS-independent, confirming they are not confusability-driven. The chi-square statistic (χ²=6952) reflects massive distributional shift in error types across BCS bins. This provides the mechanistic evidence for Channel 2: confusability literally causes the model to assign the wrong type label to correctly detected entities, rather than failing to detect them.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SDGD v2 on MAVEN (168 event types)\",\n    \"description\": \"SDGD v2 (with merge-dedup) on MAVEN Event Detection. 168 event types partitioned into low-confusability batches via graph coloring. Compare vs single-pass, random batching v2, and per-type splitting v2.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"single_pass\": {\n        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.247,\n        \"exact_f1\": 0.018,\n        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.268,\n        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.229,\n        \"batches\": 1,\n        \"time_s\": 82\n      },\n      \"sdgd_v2\": {\n        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.282,\n        \"exact_f1\": 0.028,\n        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.258,\n        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.312,\n        \"batches\": 12,\n        \"time_s\": 485,\n        \"f1_delta_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+3.5\",\n        \"precision_drop_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"-1.0\"\n      },\n      \"random_v2\": {\n        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.265,\n        \"exact_f1\": 0.022,\n        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.251,\n        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.281,\n        \"batches\": 12,\n        \"time_s\": 510\n      },\n      \"per_type_v2\": {\n        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.291,\n        \"exact_f1\": 0.031,\n        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.261,\n        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.329,\n        \"batches\": 168,\n        \"time_s\": 6250\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"MAVEN SDGD v2 Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"relaxed_F1\",\n          \"exact_F1\",\n          \"precision\",\n          \"recall\",\n          \"batches\",\n          \"time_s\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"single_pass\",\n            0.247,\n            0.018,\n            0.268,\n            0.229,\n            1,\n            82\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sdgd_v2\",\n            0.282,\n            0.028,\n            0.258,\n            0.312,\n            12,\n            485\n          ],\n          [\n            \"random_v2\",\n            0.265,\n            0.022,\n            0.251,\n            0.281,\n            12,\n            510\n          ],\n          [\n            \"per_type_v2\",\n            0.291,\n            0.031,\n            0.261,\n            0.329,\n            168,\n            6250\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"MAVEN replication confirms SDGD v2 generalizes beyond NER. The key insight is that merge-dedup converts decomposition's recall boost into net F1 gain by controlling precision loss. On MAVEN specifically, the improvement is driven by better separation of FrameNet-sibling event types that single-pass conflates. Cross-task (NER+ED) SDGD v2 success strengthens Claim 3.\\n\\n\\nMAVEN's 168 event types create a dense confusability graph with many FrameNet-sibling clusters (e.g., \\\"Attack\\\" vs \\\"Catastrophe\\\" vs \\\"Destroying\\\"). SDGD v2's graph coloring separates these siblings across batches, reducing within-batch type confusion. The merge-dedup strategy is critical: SDGD-zero failed because multi-batch merging without dedup amplified false positives. Merge-dedup resolves this by (a) deduplicating overlapping trigger predictions across batches using span matching, and (b) selecting the highest-confidence prediction when conflicts arise. The +3.5pp relaxed F1 gain reflects genuine confusion reduction. The precision drop (-1.0pp) is within the ≤5pp budget because merge-dedup catches most spurious duplicates. The gain is larger than on Few-NERD (+3.5pp vs +3.5pp relaxed) in absolute terms because MAVEN's 168 types provide more room for decomposition: more confusable pairs to separate, and the single-pass baseline is more degraded. Random batching v2 captures partial benefit (batch reduction + dedup) but misses confusability-aware separation.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (Empirical Calibration)\",\n    \"description\": \"SDGD-cal v2: confusability graph from 5-10 calibration examples (empirical confusion matrix) instead of embedding SCS. Compare vs SDGD-zero v2 and single-pass on Few-NERD.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sdgd_cal_v2\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.493,\n        \"precision\": 0.548,\n        \"recall\": 0.449,\n        \"batches\": 8,\n        \"time_s\": 405,\n        \"calibration_calls\": 660,\n        \"calibration_time_s\": 320\n      },\n      \"sdgd_zero_v2\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.48,\n        \"precision\": 0.538,\n        \"recall\": 0.433\n      },\n      \"single_pass\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.445,\n        \"precision\": 0.563,\n        \"recall\": 0.368\n      },\n      \"deltas\": {\n        \"sdgd_cal_vs_zero_pp\": \"+1.3\",\n        \"sdgd_cal_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+4.8\"\n      },\n      \"calibration_vs_embedding\": {\n        \"spearman_rho_scs_vs_empirical\": 0.42,\n        \"kendall_tau_scs_vs_empirical\": 0.3,\n        \"n_pair_rank_disagreements_top50\": 11\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Few-NERD SDGD-cal vs SDGD-zero\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"precision\",\n          \"recall\",\n          \"Δ_vs_single\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"single_pass\",\n            0.445,\n            0.563,\n            0.368,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sdgd_zero_v2\",\n            0.48,\n            0.538,\n            0.433,\n            \"+3.5pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sdgd_cal_v2\",\n            0.493,\n            0.548,\n            0.449,\n            \"+4.8pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"SDGD-cal establishes an upper bound for embedding-free SDGD. The ρ=0.42 correlation between SCS and empirical confusion validates the embedding proxy while showing room for improvement. In practice, SDGD-cal is recommended when calibration data is available; SDGD-zero when it is not. Both outperform single-pass, confirming Claim 3.\\n\\n\\nEmpirical calibration measures actual model confusion on held-out examples, capturing model-specific biases that embedding SCS approximates but cannot fully predict. For example, Qwen3 may confuse \\\"building-library\\\" ↔ \\\"building-other\\\" more than their embedding similarity suggests (due to prompt formatting or training distribution), and conversely may handle \\\"person-politician\\\" ↔ \\\"person-scholar\\\" better than expected. These rank disagreements (11/50 in top-50 pairs) are enough to reshuffle the confusability graph and produce marginally better batch assignments. The +1.3pp gain over SDGD-zero is modest because (a) the embedding proxy is already ρ=0.42 correlated with empirical confusion, capturing the dominant similarity axis, and (b) Few-NERD's cross-domain schema has clear cluster boundaries that both methods identify. The calibration overhead (660 API calls, ~5min) is acceptable for offline schema decomposition planning. The main value of SDGD-cal is that it bypasses embedding choice entirely — critical for practitioners who cannot determine which embedding best suits their schema a priori.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SDGD-cal on MAVEN (Empirical Calibration, 168 types)\",\n    \"description\": \"SDGD-cal v2 on MAVEN with empirical confusion matrix from calibration examples. Full comparison table: single-pass / SDGD-zero v2 / SDGD-cal v2 / random v2 / per-type v2.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sdgd_cal_v2\": {\n        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.298,\n        \"exact_f1\": 0.032,\n        \"precision_relaxed\": 0.264,\n        \"recall_relaxed\": 0.342,\n        \"batches\": 12,\n        \"calibration_calls\": 1680,\n        \"calibration_time_s\": 810\n      },\n      \"sdgd_zero_v2\": {\n        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.282,\n        \"exact_f1\": 0.028\n      },\n      \"single_pass\": {\n        \"relaxed_f1\": 0.247,\n        \"exact_f1\": 0.018\n      },\n      \"deltas\": {\n        \"sdgd_cal_vs_zero_pp\": \"+1.6\",\n        \"sdgd_cal_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+5.1\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"MAVEN Full Comparison Table\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"relaxed_F1\",\n          \"exact_F1\",\n          \"batches\",\n          \"total_time_s\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"single_pass\",\n            0.247,\n            0.018,\n            1,\n            82\n          ],\n          [\n            \"random_v2\",\n            0.265,\n            0.022,\n            12,\n            510\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sdgd_zero_v2\",\n            0.282,\n            0.028,\n            12,\n            485\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sdgd_cal_v2\",\n            0.298,\n            0.032,\n            12,\n            1295\n          ],\n          [\n            \"per_type_v2\",\n            0.291,\n            0.031,\n            168,\n            6250\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Notably, SDGD-cal v2 (0.298) slightly exceeds per-type v2 (0.291) on MAVEN. This is because per-type splitting eliminates within-batch confusion but loses ALL cross-type context, while SDGD-cal retains beneficial cross-type context (non-confusable types in the same batch help the model discriminate). This inverted result — SDGD-cal > per-type — is a strong argument for confusability-AWARE decomposition over naive per-type splitting. It also addresses the UniNER comparison directly.\\n\\n\\nSDGD-cal's advantage over SDGD-zero is slightly larger on MAVEN (+1.6pp vs +1.3pp on Few-NERD) because MAVEN's homogeneous FrameNet-based schema makes embedding similarity less discriminative. Many event type pairs have uniformly high SCS (mean=0.216, narrow distribution), so the embedding proxy struggles to rank confusability among mid-range pairs. Empirical calibration captures within-cluster confusion structure that embedding cosine similarity compresses: \\\"Attack\\\" and \\\"Catastrophe\\\" may have similar SCS to \\\"Attack\\\" and \\\"Protest\\\", but the actual confusion rates differ substantially due to trigger word overlap patterns. The 1680 calibration calls (168 types × 10 examples) are the main cost, but acceptable for a one-time schema analysis. The +5.1pp gain over single-pass on MAVEN is the largest SDGD improvement across all experiments, reflecting MAVEN's 168-type schema as the highest-complexity setting.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Full replication of exp-002 protocol with LLaMA-3.3-70B-Instruct on Few-NERD. Count curve + BCS-controlled batches + pairwise analysis + per-type analysis.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"count_curve\": {\n        \"count_1\": {\n          \"f1\": 0.351,\n          \"precision\": 0.248,\n          \"recall\": 0.598\n        },\n        \"count_6\": {\n          \"f1\": 0.328,\n          \"precision\": 0.258,\n          \"recall\": 0.451\n        },\n        \"count_12\": {\n          \"f1\": 0.364,\n          \"precision\": 0.396,\n          \"recall\": 0.337\n        },\n        \"count_22\": {\n          \"f1\": 0.318,\n          \"precision\": 0.372,\n          \"recall\": 0.278\n        },\n        \"count_44\": {\n          \"f1\": 0.432,\n          \"precision\": 0.51,\n          \"recall\": 0.375\n        },\n        \"count_64\": {\n          \"f1\": 0.408,\n          \"precision\": 0.625,\n          \"recall\": 0.302\n        }\n      },\n      \"pairwise_analysis\": {\n        \"all_pairs\": {\n          \"n_pairs\": 1953,\n          \"pearson_r\": 0.138,\n          \"pearson_p\": 2.1e-09,\n          \"spearman_rho\": 0.142,\n          \"spearman_p\": 8.4e-10\n        },\n        \"nonzero_only\": {\n          \"spearman_rho\": 0.221,\n          \"spearman_p\": 3.5e-05\n        },\n        \"count_12\": {\n          \"pearson_r\": 0.129,\n          \"pearson_p\": 1.8e-08\n        }\n      },\n      \"extreme_batch_confusion\": {\n        \"count_6\": {\n          \"high_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.058,\n          \"low_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.003,\n          \"ratio\": 19.3\n        },\n        \"count_12\": {\n          \"high_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.041,\n          \"low_bcs_confusion_rate\": 0.005,\n          \"ratio\": 8.2\n        }\n      },\n      \"bcs_controlled\": {\n        \"count_6_low_bcs_f1\": 0.358,\n        \"count_6_high_bcs_f1\": 0.271,\n        \"count_6_delta_pp\": -8.7,\n        \"count_12_low_bcs_f1\": 0.391,\n        \"count_12_high_bcs_f1\": 0.286,\n        \"count_12_delta_pp\": -10.5\n      },\n      \"per_type_analysis\": {\n        \"n_types\": 63,\n        \"spearman_rho\": -0.248,\n        \"spearman_p\": 0.049,\n        \"partial_r_ctrl_logfreq\": -0.172,\n        \"partial_p\": 0.178\n      },\n      \"cross_model_comparison\": {\n        \"qwen3_pairwise_r\": 0.156,\n        \"llama_pairwise_r\": 0.138,\n        \"qwen3_confusion_ratio_range\": \"10-28x\",\n        \"llama_confusion_ratio_range\": \"8-19x\",\n        \"qwen3_bcs_delta_count12\": -10.1,\n        \"llama_bcs_delta_count12\": -10.5\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-Model Pairwise Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"Qwen3-32B\",\n          \"LLaMA-70B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"pairwise Pearson r\",\n            0.156,\n            0.138\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pairwise p-value\",\n            \"4.6e-12\",\n            \"2.1e-09\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"nonzero Spearman ρ\",\n            0.254,\n            0.221\n          ],\n          [\n            \"confusion ratio (count_6 extreme)\",\n            \"27.5x\",\n            \"19.3x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"confusion ratio (count_12 extreme)\",\n            \"9.9x\",\n            \"8.2x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"BCS-controlled Δ (count_12)\",\n            \"-10.1pp\",\n            \"-10.5pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"per-type ρ\",\n            -0.293,\n            -0.248\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"LLaMA Count Curve\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"count\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"precision\",\n          \"recall\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            0.351,\n            0.248,\n            0.598\n          ],\n          [\n            6,\n            0.328,\n            0.258,\n            0.451\n          ],\n          [\n            12,\n            0.364,\n            0.396,\n            0.337\n          ],\n          [\n            22,\n            0.318,\n            0.372,\n            0.278\n          ],\n          [\n            44,\n            0.432,\n            0.51,\n            0.375\n          ],\n          [\n            64,\n            0.408,\n            0.625,\n            0.302\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cross-model validation is the single most important experiment for reviewer concern mitigation. Both models agree on direction, significance, and approximate magnitude of the pairwise confusability effect. The residual differences (r=0.156 vs 0.138, ratio 28x vs 19x) are consistent with model capacity differences rather than contradictory findings. This eliminates the \\\"model-specific artifact\\\" concern and allows the paper to claim the confusability signal is a property of the task structure, not the model.\\n\\n\\nLLaMA-3.3-70B exhibits the same pairwise confusability signal as Qwen3-32B because the underlying mechanism — semantically similar co-present types cause type interference during label assignment — is architecture-independent. Both models are decoder-only transformers that process type labels as natural language tokens; when two labels share semantic content, the model's next-token prediction distributes probability mass across both, increasing confusion. The effect size is slightly attenuated (r=0.138 vs 0.156) because LLaMA-70B's larger parameter count provides marginally better type discrimination capacity. The confusion ratio (8-19x) is in the same order as Qwen3 (10-28x) but lower because the 70B model makes fewer type confusion errors per entity overall. The per-type signal is weaker (ρ=-0.248, borderline p=0.049) because per-type aggregation introduces noise and LLaMA's different tokenization creates slight rank perturbations. Critically, the pairwise correlation (r=0.138, p=2.1e-9) is highly significant and falls within the best-tier range [0.10, 0.20], confirming the signal is not model-specific. The BCS-controlled delta (-10.5pp at count_12) falls within the cross-model range [-35, -15]pp when scaled to larger schemas.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Random Batching Ablation (Same k as SDGD)\",\n    \"description\": \"SDGD v2 vs random batching v2 (same batch count k=8) on Few-NERD. Both use merge-dedup. Isolates confusability-aware grouping from batch count reduction.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sdgd_v2\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.48,\n        \"precision\": 0.538,\n        \"recall\": 0.433,\n        \"batches\": 8,\n        \"time_s\": 395,\n        \"std_across_5_seeds\": 0.008\n      },\n      \"random_v2\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.458,\n        \"precision\": 0.521,\n        \"recall\": 0.411,\n        \"batches\": 8,\n        \"time_s\": 410,\n        \"std_across_5_seeds\": 0.015\n      },\n      \"single_pass\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.445,\n        \"precision\": 0.563,\n        \"recall\": 0.368\n      },\n      \"deltas\": {\n        \"sdgd_v2_vs_random_v2_pp\": \"+2.2\",\n        \"random_v2_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+1.3\",\n        \"sdgd_v2_vs_single_pass_pp\": \"+3.5\"\n      },\n      \"decomposition_attribution\": {\n        \"batch_reduction_contribution_pp\": 1.3,\n        \"confusability_aware_grouping_pp\": 2.2,\n        \"total_sdgd_gain_pp\": 3.5,\n        \"pct_from_grouping\": 62.9\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Ablation: Grouping vs Batch Reduction\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"Δ_vs_single\",\n          \"σ(5 seeds)\",\n          \"source_of_gain\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"single_pass\",\n            0.445,\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"random_v2 (k=8)\",\n            0.458,\n            \"+1.3pp\",\n            0.015,\n            \"batch reduction only\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sdgd_v2 (k=8)\",\n            0.48,\n            \"+3.5pp\",\n            0.008,\n            \"batch reduction + grouping\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This ablation is essential for Claim 3: it proves SDGD's value is primarily from confusability-aware grouping (63%), not mere batch reduction. Without this ablation, a reviewer could argue that any decomposition strategy would work equally well. The 2.2pp gap, combined with SDGD's lower variance, makes a clean case for principled decomposition.\\n\\n\\nMerge-dedup resolves the core precision problem for ALL decomposition strategies, so both random and SDGD benefit equally from dedup. The remaining difference (2.2pp) isolates the value of confusability-aware batch composition. Random batching gets 1.3pp from batch reduction alone (smaller batches → better per-entity attention → higher recall, with dedup controlling precision). SDGD adds 2.2pp by ensuring confusable type pairs are separated, reducing within-batch type confusion. The 62.9% attribution to grouping (vs 37.1% to batch reduction) quantifies SDGD's specific contribution. SDGD also shows lower variance (σ=0.008 vs 0.015) because its batch composition is deterministic and optimized, while random batching quality fluctuates depending on which confusable types happen to co-occur. The lower variance is practically important — SDGD gives predictable quality.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Per-Type Splitting Ablation (UniNER-style)\",\n    \"description\": \"Per-type splitting v2 (1 type per batch, k=66) on Few-NERD with merge-dedup. Upper bound on decomposition benefit. Quality-latency tradeoff vs SDGD v2.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"per_type_v2\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.492,\n        \"precision\": 0.545,\n        \"recall\": 0.449,\n        \"batches\": 66,\n        \"time_s\": 2480,\n        \"api_calls\": 66\n      },\n      \"sdgd_v2\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.48,\n        \"precision\": 0.538,\n        \"recall\": 0.433,\n        \"batches\": 8,\n        \"time_s\": 395,\n        \"api_calls\": 8\n      },\n      \"single_pass\": {\n        \"f1\": 0.445,\n        \"precision\": 0.563,\n        \"recall\": 0.368,\n        \"batches\": 1,\n        \"time_s\": 78,\n        \"api_calls\": 1\n      },\n      \"quality_vs_latency\": {\n        \"sdgd_v2_quality_pct_of_per_type\": 97.6,\n        \"sdgd_v2_latency_pct_of_per_type\": 15.9,\n        \"sdgd_v2_api_call_pct_of_per_type\": 12.1\n      },\n      \"per_type_vs_sdgd_delta_pp\": \"+1.2\",\n      \"residual_confusion_analysis\": {\n        \"sdgd_residual_confusion_pairs\": 23,\n        \"mean_scs_of_residual\": 0.612,\n        \"max_scs_of_residual\": 0.743\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Quality-Latency Pareto Frontier\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"batches\",\n          \"time_s\",\n          \"F1_per_minute\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"single_pass\",\n            0.445,\n            1,\n            78,\n            0.342\n          ],\n          [\n            \"sdgd_v2\",\n            0.48,\n            8,\n            395,\n            0.073\n          ],\n          [\n            \"per_type_v2\",\n            0.492,\n            66,\n            2480,\n            0.012\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The per-type ablation addresses the UniNER comparison directly. UniNER-style per-type splitting provides the highest quality but at prohibitive cost for real-time applications. SDGD v2 offers a principled middle ground — it captures 97.6% of the quality with a data-driven criterion for WHICH types need separation. This is the operational contribution: not just \\\"decompose the schema\\\" but \\\"decompose it optimally given a latency budget.\\\"\\n\\n\\nPer-type splitting eliminates within-batch type confusion entirely — with one type per batch, there is no co-present type to confuse with. Merge-dedup handles cross-batch deduplication. The resulting F1 (0.492) is the theoretical ceiling for decomposition-based approaches. SDGD v2 achieves 97.6% of this ceiling at 15.9% of the latency because graph coloring concentrates separation effort on the most confusable pairs (SCS > 0.8), while allowing low-similarity types to share batches safely. The residual 1.2pp gap comes from 23 moderately confusable pairs (mean SCS=0.612) that SDGD groups together to keep k=8. These pairs cause occasional type confusion that per-type avoids entirely. The tradeoff is clear: 1.2pp F1 costs 6.3x latency and 8.25x API calls. For production deployment, SDGD v2 dominates per-type splitting on cost-effectiveness. For offline analysis where latency is irrelevant, per-type v2 provides the best quality.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SCS Proxy Validation (Embedding vs Empirical Confusion)\",\n    \"description\": \"Rank correlation between embedding SCS and empirical confusion matrix. Validates embedding proxy for SDGD-zero practical use.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"fewnerd_qwen3\": {\n        \"spearman_rho\": 0.43,\n        \"kendall_tau\": 0.31,\n        \"n_pairs_with_confusion\": 287,\n        \"top10_overlap\": 7\n      },\n      \"maven_minilm\": {\n        \"spearman_rho\": 0.38,\n        \"kendall_tau\": 0.27,\n        \"n_pairs_with_confusion\": 1842,\n        \"top10_overlap\": 6\n      },\n      \"maven_qwen3\": {\n        \"spearman_rho\": 0.15,\n        \"kendall_tau\": 0.11,\n        \"n_pairs_with_confusion\": 1842,\n        \"top10_overlap\": 2\n      },\n      \"fewnerd_minilm\": {\n        \"spearman_rho\": 0.22,\n        \"kendall_tau\": 0.16,\n        \"n_pairs_with_confusion\": 287,\n        \"top10_overlap\": 4\n      },\n      \"cross_validation\": {\n        \"best_embedding_per_dataset\": {\n          \"fewnerd\": \"Qwen3 (ρ=0.43)\",\n          \"maven\": \"MiniLM (ρ=0.38)\"\n        },\n        \"pattern\": \"extraction-model embeddings excel on cross-domain schemas; generic embeddings excel on homogeneous schemas\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"SCS Proxy Validation Matrix\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"dataset\",\n          \"embedding\",\n          \"Spearman_ρ\",\n          \"Kendall_τ\",\n          \"top10_overlap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Few-NERD\",\n            \"Qwen3\",\n            0.43,\n            0.31,\n            \"7/10\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Few-NERD\",\n            \"MiniLM\",\n            0.22,\n            0.16,\n            \"4/10\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MAVEN\",\n            \"MiniLM\",\n            0.38,\n            0.27,\n            \"6/10\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MAVEN\",\n            \"Qwen3\",\n            0.15,\n            0.11,\n            \"2/10\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Validates SDGD-zero as a practical no-calibration method while explaining why SDGD-cal improves: the ρ=0.43 ceiling for embedding-based proxy means ~18% of variance in confusion is captured, leaving room for empirical calibration. The cross-embedding pattern provides practical guidance: use extraction-model embeddings for diverse schemas, generic embeddings for domain-specific schemas, or SDGD-cal to bypass the choice entirely. This completes the SDGD framework: SDGD-zero for zero-cost deployment, SDGD-cal for maximum quality.\\n\\nThe SCS-confusion correlation (ρ=0.38-0.43 for best embedding per dataset) validates the embedding proxy as a reasonable but imperfect predictor of actual model confusion. The imperfection is expected: embedding SCS captures semantic similarity (the dominant confusion axis) but not model-specific factors like tokenization artifacts, prompt position effects, or label frequency biases. The 2×2 pattern (Qwen3 better for Few-NERD, MiniLM better for MAVEN) replicates the D010 finding at the validated pairwise level. For Few-NERD's cross-domain schema, Qwen3 embeddings align with the extraction model's internal representations, capturing model-specific confusability. For MAVEN's homogeneous FrameNet schema, Qwen3 compresses within-domain distinctions while MiniLM's generic sentence embedding preserves them. The top-10 overlap (6-7/10) shows that both best-embedding conditions identify the most confusable pairs with reasonable fidelity, validating SDGD-zero's practical utility. The gap between ρ=0.43 and a hypothetical perfect proxy (ρ=1.0) explains SDGD-cal's incremental gain.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":978,"startLine":1,"totalLines":978}}}
